IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `qcat-dates`

-- Queensland Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2009 -- the provisions a person bringing a claim or application to
-- the tribunal (QCAT) meets, from the seat of THE APPLICANT.
--
-- SEAT: an individual who brings a claim to QCAT -- a minor civil dispute (a debt, a consumer claim against a trader, a
-- claim for damage to property caused by a vehicle, a tenancy matter, a dividing fences or trees dispute), an
-- application in another original jurisdiction, or an application to review a government decision -- and who may later
-- need to enforce, reopen or appeal the tribunal's decision. "Is my claim a minor civil dispute?", "By when must I
-- apply?", "Can I have a lawyer?", "Can I get a decision by default?", "Can I be made to pay costs?", "How do I enforce
-- it?", "Can I appeal, and by when?". The respondent and the decision-maker meet the same rules from the other side;
-- the tribunal, its registrar and the Court of Appeal get no seat. Every power of the tribunal that turns on its view
-- is answered conditionally: the view is asked as a fact, never decided.
--
-- Every exported question takes plain facts (yes/no, dates, numbers, a choice from a list). Four time limits are DEONTIC
-- rules run inside (asking a decision-maker for reasons, applying for review, asking the tribunal for written reasons,
-- filing an appeal or an application for leave to appeal); no question takes a DEONTIC or an EVENT.
--
-- Unofficial version. Based on content from the Queensland Legislation website at 6 November 2026. (c) The State of
-- Queensland, CC BY 4.0. See SOURCE-LICENSE.md.
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Queensland Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2009: the questions of an applicant`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `The parties, the acts and the time rules`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- Schedule 3: "applicant means (a) for an application or a proceeding to be started on application--the person who
-- makes the application". Only the applicant's own acts are traced; the other side's are asked as facts.
DECLARE `Party` IS ONE OF
    `I`

DECLARE `Act done` IS ONE OF
    `ask the decision-maker for a written statement of reasons`
    `file my application for review`
    `ask the tribunal for written reasons`
    `file my appeal or application for leave to appeal`

-- Runs a rule for me over one act done, at a time counted in days from the rule's start (a time before the start counts
-- as the start). On the stable l4 a CONSIDER on an EVALTRACE can match FULFILLED but not BREACH. It takes a DEONTIC
-- value, so it is never exported. An act on the last day of the period is in time.
GIVEN `the rule` IS A DEONTIC `Party` `Act done`
      `the act` IS AN `Act done`
      `the time it was done` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `done in time under` `the rule` `the act` `at` `the time it was done` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE `the rule` 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the time counted`), (EVENT `I` `the act` `the time counted`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE
    WHERE
        `the time counted` MEANS
            IF `the time it was done` LESS THAN 0
            THEN 0
            ELSE `the time it was done`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 11, 11A, 12, 13, 14 and schedule 3 -- minor civil disputes`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- Schedule 3: "prescribed amount means $25000."
`the prescribed amount` MEANS 25000

-- Schedule 3: "consumer means an individual-- (a) who buys or hires goods other than-- (i) for resale or letting on
-- hire; or (ii) in a trade or business carried on by the individual; or (iii) as a member of a business partnership; or
-- (b) for whom services are supplied for fee or reward other than-- (i) in a trade or business carried on by the
-- individual; or (ii) as a member of a business partnership; or (c) who is or was the tenant of premises let to the
-- individual as a dwelling other than for-- (i) assigning or subletting the premises to someone else; or (ii) a trade or
-- business carried on by the individual." A "means" definition: JUST FALSE kind when it fails.
DECLARE `What the contract gave me` IS ONE OF
    `goods I bought or hired`
    `services supplied to me for a fee or reward`
    `premises let to me as a dwelling`
    `something else`

GIVEN `I am an individual` IS A BOOLEAN
      `what I got` IS A `What the contract gave me`
      `for business` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `schedule 3 -- I am a consumer` `I am an individual` `what I got` `for business` IF
        `I am an individual`
    AND (NOT (`for business`))
    AND (CONSIDER `what I got`
         WHEN `something else` THEN FALSE
         OTHERWISE TRUE)

-- Schedule 3: "trader-- 1 A trader-- (a) means a person who in trade or commerce-- (i) carries on a business of
-- supplying goods or providing services; or (ii) regularly holds himself, herself or itself out as ready to supply goods
-- or to provide services of a similar nature; and (b) includes a person who is or was the landlord of premises let to a
-- tenant as a dwelling ... 2 However, a person is not a trader in relation to goods or services if in supplying the
-- goods or providing the services-- (a) the person acts in the exercise of a discipline that is not ordinarily regarded
-- as within the field of trade or commerce; or (b) [acts on such a person's instructions]." The two limbs of
-- paragraph 1(a) are asked together; paragraph 2's two limbs are asked together.
GIVEN `in business supplying` IS A BOOLEAN
      `landlord of a dwelling` IS A BOOLEAN
      `outside trade or commerce` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `schedule 3 -- a trader` `in business supplying` `landlord of a dwelling` `outside trade or commerce` IF
        (`in business supplying` OR `landlord of a dwelling`)
    AND (NOT (`outside trade or commerce`))

DECLARE `Kind of claim` IS ONE OF
    `a claim to recover a debt or liquidated demand of money`
    `a claim arising out of a contract for goods or services, or a lease of a dwelling`
    `a claim for damage to property caused by, or arising out of the use of, a vehicle`
    `a tenancy matter under the Residential Tenancies and Rooming Accommodation Act 2008`
    `a dividing fences or trees dispute under the Neighbourhood Disputes (Dividing Fences and Trees) Act 2011, chapter 2`
    `a pool barrier matter under the Building Act 1975, chapter 8, part 2A`
    `another kind of claim`

DECLARE `Is it a minor civil dispute` IS ONE OF
    `yes: it is a minor civil dispute (schedule 3, paragraph 1), and you may apply to the tribunal to hear and decide it (ss 11, 12(1))`
    `yes, but only if you agree to limit your claim to $25,000: applying to the tribunal to deal with it as a minor civil dispute limits it, and you give up the rest (s 12(2), (3))`
    `no: not one of the kinds of claim in schedule 3, paragraph 1 (definition of minor civil dispute)`
    `no: an enabling Act gives the tribunal jurisdiction over this debt claim and does not expressly say it is a minor civil dispute (schedule 3, paragraph 2)`
    `no: a claim under the Fair Work Act 2009 (Cwlth), section 539 is not a minor civil dispute (schedule 3, paragraph 4)`
    `no: a claim in a proceeding to which the Fair Trading Act 1989, section 50A applies is not a minor civil dispute (schedule 3, paragraph 3)`
    `no: only the consumer (under a contract between a consumer and a trader) or a trader (under a contract between traders) may bring this contract claim as a minor civil dispute (schedule 3, paragraph 1(b); s 12(1), (4)(b), (c))`
    `no: you are not the person who may apply to the tribunal for this kind of claim (s 12(1), (4))`

-- Schedule 3, minor civil dispute: "1 Minor civil dispute means-- (a) a claim to recover a debt or liquidated demand
-- of money of up to the prescribed amount; or (b) a claim arising out of a contract between a consumer and trader, or a
-- contract between 2 or more traders, that is-- (i) for payment of money of a value not more than the prescribed amount
-- ... (v) [a combination] where the total value of the combined claim is not more than the prescribed amount; or (c) a
-- claim for an amount of not more than the prescribed amount for damage to property caused by, or arising out of the use
-- of, a vehicle; or (d) a tenancy matter; or (e) [a Neighbourhood Disputes Act chapter 2 claim] for an amount not more
-- than the prescribed amount; or (f) [a Building Act 1975 chapter 8 part 2A matter]. 2 However, if an enabling Act
-- confers jurisdiction on the tribunal to deal with a claim ... within the meaning of paragraph 1(a), the claim is not a
-- minor civil dispute unless the enabling Act expressly states it is ... 3 A claim mentioned in paragraph 1(b) does not
-- include a claim in a proceeding to which the Fair Trading Act 1989, section 50A applies. 4 A claim mentioned in
-- paragraph 1(a) does not include a claim under the Fair Work Act 2009 (Cwlth), section 539."
-- s 11A(1): interest "must not be considered in calculating whether an amount ... is more than the monetary limit".
-- s 12(2) "A relevant person may ... agree to limit the person's claim to the prescribed amount in order to bring the
-- claim within the tribunal's jurisdiction for a minor civil dispute." (3) by applying to deal with it as a minor civil
-- dispute. (4) relevant person: (a) for a debt, the person to whom it is owed; (b) "subject to paragraphs (c) to (f), for
-- a claim arising out of a contract between a consumer and a trader--the consumer"; (c) between traders, any of them;
-- (d) for vehicle damage, "a person incurring loss because of the damage"; (e) tenancy, a person who may apply under the
-- RTRA Act; (f) fences and trees, a party to the dispute; (g) pool barriers, a person who may apply under the Building
-- Act. s 12(1): the tribunal may exercise the jurisdiction "if a relevant person has ... applied": JUST FALSE kind when
-- the applicant is not one. A tenancy matter and a pool barrier matter have no amount in the definition (fork F1).
GIVEN `the kind of claim` IS A `Kind of claim`
      `the amount claimed` IS A NUMBER
      `I may apply` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I am an individual` IS A BOOLEAN
      `what I got` IS A `What the contract gave me`
      `for business` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I am in business supplying` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the other party is in business supplying` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the other party is my landlord` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the other party acts outside trade or commerce` IS A BOOLEAN
      `an enabling Act covers this debt` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a Fair Work Act s 539 claim` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a Fair Trading Act s 50A proceeding` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN `Is it a minor civil dispute`
`schedule 3 and s 12 -- a minor civil dispute` `the kind of claim` `the amount claimed` `I may apply` `I am an individual` `what I got` `for business` `I am in business supplying` `the other party is in business supplying` `the other party is my landlord` `the other party acts outside trade or commerce` `an enabling Act covers this debt` `a Fair Work Act s 539 claim` `a Fair Trading Act s 50A proceeding` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the kind of claim`
    WHEN `a claim to recover a debt or liquidated demand of money` THEN
        BRANCH
            IF `an enabling Act covers this debt`
            THEN `no: an enabling Act gives the tribunal jurisdiction over this debt claim and does not expressly say it is a minor civil dispute (schedule 3, paragraph 2)`
            IF `a Fair Work Act s 539 claim`
            THEN `no: a claim under the Fair Work Act 2009 (Cwlth), section 539 is not a minor civil dispute (schedule 3, paragraph 4)`
            IF NOT (`I may apply`)
            THEN `no: you are not the person who may apply to the tribunal for this kind of claim (s 12(1), (4))`
            OTHERWISE `within the limit`
    WHEN `a claim arising out of a contract for goods or services, or a lease of a dwelling` THEN
        BRANCH
            IF `a Fair Trading Act s 50A proceeding`
            THEN `no: a claim in a proceeding to which the Fair Trading Act 1989, section 50A applies is not a minor civil dispute (schedule 3, paragraph 3)`
            IF `I am a consumer and the other a trader` OR `we are both traders`
            THEN `within the limit`
            OTHERWISE `no: only the consumer (under a contract between a consumer and a trader) or a trader (under a contract between traders) may bring this contract claim as a minor civil dispute (schedule 3, paragraph 1(b); s 12(1), (4)(b), (c))`
    WHEN `a claim for damage to property caused by, or arising out of the use of, a vehicle` THEN `if I may apply, within the limit`
    WHEN `a tenancy matter under the Residential Tenancies and Rooming Accommodation Act 2008` THEN `if I may apply, yes`
    WHEN `a dividing fences or trees dispute under the Neighbourhood Disputes (Dividing Fences and Trees) Act 2011, chapter 2` THEN `if I may apply, within the limit`
    WHEN `a pool barrier matter under the Building Act 1975, chapter 8, part 2A` THEN `if I may apply, yes`
    WHEN `another kind of claim` THEN `no: not one of the kinds of claim in schedule 3, paragraph 1 (definition of minor civil dispute)`
    WHERE
        `the other party is a trader` MEANS
            `schedule 3 -- a trader` `the other party is in business supplying` `the other party is my landlord` `the other party acts outside trade or commerce`
        `I am a consumer and the other a trader` MEANS
                `schedule 3 -- I am a consumer` `I am an individual` `what I got` `for business`
            AND `the other party is a trader`
        `we are both traders` MEANS
                `schedule 3 -- a trader` `I am in business supplying` FALSE FALSE
            AND `the other party is a trader`
        `within the limit` MEANS
            IF `the amount claimed` AT MOST `the prescribed amount`
            THEN `yes: it is a minor civil dispute (schedule 3, paragraph 1), and you may apply to the tribunal to hear and decide it (ss 11, 12(1))`
            ELSE `yes, but only if you agree to limit your claim to $25,000: applying to the tribunal to deal with it as a minor civil dispute limits it, and you give up the rest (s 12(2), (3))`
        `if I may apply, within the limit` MEANS
            IF `I may apply`
            THEN `within the limit`
            ELSE `no: you are not the person who may apply to the tribunal for this kind of claim (s 12(1), (4))`
        `if I may apply, yes` MEANS
            IF `I may apply`
            THEN `yes: it is a minor civil dispute (schedule 3, paragraph 1), and you may apply to the tribunal to hear and decide it (ss 11, 12(1))`
            ELSE `no: you are not the person who may apply to the tribunal for this kind of claim (s 12(1), (4))`

@export Is my claim a minor civil dispute that the Queensland Civil and Administrative Tribunal (QCAT) can hear, under the QCAT Act 2009 (ss 11, 11A, 12 and the schedule 3 definitions of minor civil dispute, consumer and trader)? Minor civil disputes include debts, consumer and trader contract claims and vehicle property damage claims of up to $25,000 (interest not counted), tenancy matters, and some fence, tree and pool barrier disputes. A larger claim can be brought if you agree to limit it to $25,000.
GIVEN `the kind of claim` IS A `Kind of claim` @desc What your claim is about. One of: a debt or liquidated (fixed) amount of money owed to you; a claim arising out of a contract for goods or services, or a lease of a dwelling; damage to property caused by, or arising out of the use of, a vehicle; a tenancy matter under the Residential Tenancies and Rooming Accommodation Act 2008; a dividing fences or trees dispute under the Neighbourhood Disputes (Dividing Fences and Trees) Act 2011, chapter 2; a pool barrier matter under the Building Act 1975, chapter 8, part 2A; another kind of claim
      `the amount claimed` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount or value of your claim in Australian dollars, not counting interest (for a contract claim, the total of the money, relief from payment, rectification work and goods claimed)
      `I may apply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a debt: whether the money is owed to you. For vehicle damage: whether you incurred loss because of the damage. For a tenancy matter: whether the Residential Tenancies and Rooming Accommodation Act 2008 lets you apply. For a fences or trees dispute: whether you are a party to it. For a pool barrier matter: whether the Building Act 1975 lets you apply. (Not used for contract claims.)
      `I am an individual` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a contract claim: whether you are an individual (not a company or other body)
      `what I got` IS A `What the contract gave me` @desc For a contract claim: what you got under the contract. One of: goods you bought or hired; services supplied to you for a fee or reward; premises let to you as a dwelling; something else
      `for business` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a contract claim: whether you got it for resale or letting on hire, in a trade or business you carry on, or as a member of a business partnership (for a dwelling: for assigning or subletting it, or for your trade or business)
      `I am in business supplying` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a contract claim: whether you yourself, in trade or commerce, carry on a business of supplying goods or providing services (or regularly hold yourself out as ready to), and the contract was made in that business
      `the other party is in business supplying` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a contract claim: whether the other party, in trade or commerce, carries on a business of supplying goods or providing services, or regularly holds itself out as ready to supply such goods or services
      `the other party is my landlord` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a contract claim: whether the other party is or was the landlord of premises let to you as a dwelling (not for subletting or your business)
      `the other party acts outside trade or commerce` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a contract claim: whether, in supplying the goods or services, the other party acted in a discipline not ordinarily regarded as trade or commerce (for example a profession), or on the instructions of someone who did
      `an enabling Act covers this debt` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a debt claim: whether another Act gives the tribunal jurisdiction over this kind of claim without expressly saying it is a minor civil dispute
      `a Fair Work Act s 539 claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a debt claim: whether it is a claim under the Fair Work Act 2009 (Cwlth), section 539 (for example unpaid wages under a civil remedy provision)
      `a Fair Trading Act s 50A proceeding` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a contract claim: whether it is in a proceeding to which the Fair Trading Act 1989, section 50A applies
GIVETH AN `Is it a minor civil dispute`
`Is my claim a minor civil dispute` MEANS
    `schedule 3 and s 12 -- a minor civil dispute` `the kind of claim` `the amount claimed` `I may apply` `I am an individual` `what I got` `for business` `I am in business supplying` `the other party is in business supplying` `the other party is my landlord` `the other party acts outside trade or commerce` `an enabling Act covers this debt` `a Fair Work Act s 539 claim` `a Fair Trading Act s 50A proceeding`

-- s 13(3) "the tribunal can not make an order or decision under subsection (2) that-- (a) purports to require payment of
-- an amount, performance of work or return of goods of a value of more than the prescribed amount; or (b) ... relief
-- ... of more than the prescribed amount ...; or (c) combines 2 or more orders ... of a total value of more than the
-- prescribed amount." (4) "Subsection (3) does not apply to a claim that is the subject of a dispute under the Building
-- Act 1975, chapter 8, part 2A." (5) interest is not counted. The answer is the smaller of the claim and $25,000 (fork
-- F2): the tribunal makes the orders it considers "fair and equitable" (s 13(1)), and may dismiss the application. For a
-- pool barrier matter this Act sets no cap: NOTHING.
@export What is the most the Queensland Civil and Administrative Tribunal can order in my favour in a minor civil dispute, not counting interest, under the QCAT Act 2009 s 13(3)-(5)? The cap is $25,000 (interest is not counted toward it); pool barrier matters under the Building Act 1975 have no cap under this Act.
GIVEN `the kind of claim` IS A `Kind of claim` @desc What your minor civil dispute is about (as in the question whether your claim is a minor civil dispute)
      `the amount claimed` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount or value you claim in Australian dollars, not counting interest
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`What is the most the tribunal can order in my minor civil dispute` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the kind of claim`
    WHEN `a pool barrier matter under the Building Act 1975, chapter 8, part 2A` THEN NOTHING
    WHEN `another kind of claim` THEN NOTHING
    OTHERWISE
        IF `the amount claimed` AT MOST `the prescribed amount`
        THEN JUST `the amount claimed`
        ELSE JUST `the prescribed amount`

-- s 14(1) "This section applies in relation to a minor civil dispute involving a claim to recover a debt or liquidated
-- demand of money." (2) "However, this section does not apply ... on which interest is payable as of right whether
-- because of an agreement or otherwise." (3) the tribunal "may order that there be included ... interest at the rate
-- the tribunal considers appropriate-- (a) for all or part of the amount; and (b) for all or part of the period between
-- the date when the dispute arose and the date the tribunal made its order". (4) no interest on interest.
DECLARE `Interest on a minor civil dispute` IS ONE OF
    `the tribunal may include interest, at the rate it considers appropriate, for all or part of the amount and all or part of the period from when the dispute arose to its order, but not interest on interest (s 14(3), (4))`
    `s 14 does not apply: interest is payable on this debt as of right, under an agreement or otherwise; it is not counted toward the $25,000 limit (ss 11A(1)(a), 13(5), 14(2))`
    `s 14 does not apply: it reaches only a minor civil dispute to recover a debt or liquidated demand of money (s 14(1))`

@export Can the Queensland Civil and Administrative Tribunal add interest to what I am awarded in a minor civil dispute, under the QCAT Act 2009 s 14? It can, at a rate it chooses, on a claim for a debt or a fixed amount of money, unless interest is already payable as of right (for example under the contract).
GIVEN `the kind of claim` IS A `Kind of claim` @desc What your minor civil dispute is about (a debt or liquidated demand of money, or another kind)
      `interest is payable as of right` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether interest is payable on the debt as of right, under an agreement (for example the contract or invoice terms) or a law
GIVETH AN `Interest on a minor civil dispute`
`Can the tribunal add interest to my minor civil dispute award` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the kind of claim`
    WHEN `a claim to recover a debt or liquidated demand of money` THEN
        IF `interest is payable as of right`
        THEN `s 14 does not apply: interest is payable on this debt as of right, under an agreement or otherwise; it is not counted toward the $25,000 limit (ss 11A(1)(a), 13(5), 14(2))`
        ELSE `the tribunal may include interest, at the rate it considers appropriate, for all or part of the amount and all or part of the period from when the dispute arose to its order, but not interest on interest (s 14(3), (4))`
    OTHERWISE `s 14 does not apply: it reaches only a minor civil dispute to recover a debt or liquidated demand of money (s 14(1))`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 157, 158, 159 -- reasons for a reviewable decision` -- arises only if a government decision-maker has made a decision about you that QCAT can review
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 158(1) "This section applies if a person who may apply to the tribunal for a review of a reviewable decision has not
-- been given a written statement of the reasons for the decision." (2) the person may ask. (3) "The request must be--
-- (a) in writing, whether by letter, facsimile or email; and (b) made within 14 days after the person-- (i) was notified
-- of the decision; or (ii) is ... taken to have been given notice of the decision by publication ...; or (iii) if
-- subparagraph (i) or (ii) does not apply--the day the person became aware of the decision." The three days are one
-- input. "applies if": JUST FALSE kind; a period fixed in the text: JUST FALSE outside it (ruling (a)); a request not in
-- writing: a bare "must", answered JUST FALSE (fork F4).
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Party` `Act done`
`s 158(3)(b) -- asking the decision-maker for a statement of reasons` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `I`
    DO `ask the decision-maker for a written statement of reasons`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    LEST BREACH BY `I` BECAUSE "the 14 days after notice of the decision have passed; the right to ask under section 158 is gone"

@export By when must I ask the decision-maker for a written statement of the reasons for a decision that the Queensland Civil and Administrative Tribunal can review, under the QCAT Act 2009 s 158(3)? Within 14 days after you were notified of the decision (or taken to be notified by publication, or, failing both, became aware of it).
GIVEN `the day I was notified` IS A DATE @desc The day you were notified of the decision (or were taken to be notified by publication in the gazette or a newspaper, or, if neither, the day you became aware of it); ISO 8601, e.g. 2027-03-01
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I ask the decision-maker for reasons` MEANS
    `the day` 14 `days after` `the day I was notified`

@export Can I ask the decision-maker for a written statement of the reasons for a reviewable decision, under the QCAT Act 2009 s 158? You can if you have not been given one, you ask in writing (letter, fax or email), and you ask within 14 days after you were notified of the decision.
GIVEN `I have been given a statement of reasons` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have already been given a written statement of the reasons for the decision (for example in the information notice)
      `the day I was notified` IS A DATE @desc The day you were notified of the decision (or taken to be notified by publication, or became aware of it); ISO 8601
      `my request is in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your request is in writing (letter, fax or email)
      `the day I ask` IS A DATE @desc The day you make (or made) the request; ISO 8601
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I ask the decision-maker for reasons` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `I have been given a statement of reasons`
        THEN JUST FALSE
        IF NOT (`my request is in writing`)
        THEN JUST FALSE
        OTHERWISE JUST (`done in time under` (`s 158(3)(b) -- asking the decision-maker for a statement of reasons` 14) `ask the decision-maker for a written statement of reasons` `at` (`the days from` `the day I was notified` `to` `the day I ask`))

-- s 158(4) "The decision-maker must give the person the statement within a reasonable period of not more than 28 days
-- after the request is made." The outer limit only; "reasonable" is not decided.
@export By when must the decision-maker give me the written statement of reasons I asked for, under the QCAT Act 2009 s 158(4)? Within a reasonable period, and not more than 28 days after your request. If it does not, you can apply to the tribunal for an order (s 159).
GIVEN `the day I asked` IS A DATE @desc The day you asked the decision-maker in writing for the statement of reasons; ISO 8601
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must the decision-maker give me reasons` MEANS
    `the day` 28 `days after` `the day I asked`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 33, 35, 36, 37, 38, 61 -- starting a proceeding`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 33(3) "If the application is for the review of a reviewable decision, the application must be made, by filing it in
-- the registry, within 28 days after the relevant day." (4) "relevant day ... means-- (a) the day the applicant is
-- notified of the decision; or (b) if the applicant has applied to the decision-maker for a written statement of reasons
-- for the decision under section 158--the earlier of the following days-- (i) the day the written statement is given to
-- the applicant; (ii) the day by which the written statement is required to be given to the applicant under that
-- section; or (c) if the applicant has applied to the tribunal for an order under section 159-- (i) if the tribunal makes
-- the order--the earlier of ... (A) the day the written statement of reasons the subject of the order is given ...; (B)
-- the day by which [it] is required to be given ... under the order; or (ii) if the tribunal does not make the order--the
-- day the applicant is notified of the tribunal's decision to not make the order." Paragraph (c) is taken over (b) when
-- both apply (fork F5); a s 158 request made late is not one "under section 158" (fork F6). Note 1: an enabling Act may
-- set another period (NOTHING here). While a s 159 application is undecided there is no relevant day yet: NOTHING.
DECLARE `My s 159 application` IS ONE OF
    `I have not applied for an order under s 159`
    `the tribunal made the order`
    `the tribunal refused to make the order`
    `the tribunal has not yet decided`

GIVEN `the day I was notified` IS A DATE
      `I asked for reasons in time` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day I asked` IS A DATE
      `I have been given the statement` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day I was given it` IS A DATE
      `my s 159 application` IS A `My s 159 application`
      `the day set by the order` IS A DATE
      `the day I was told of the refusal` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`s 33(4) -- the relevant day` `the day I was notified` `I asked for reasons in time` `the day I asked` `I have been given the statement` `the day I was given it` `my s 159 application` `the day set by the order` `the day I was told of the refusal` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my s 159 application`
    WHEN `the tribunal made the order` THEN JUST (`the earlier given or` `the day set by the order`)
    WHEN `the tribunal refused to make the order` THEN JUST `the day I was told of the refusal`
    WHEN `the tribunal has not yet decided` THEN NOTHING
    WHEN `I have not applied for an order under s 159` THEN
        IF `I asked for reasons in time`
        THEN JUST (`the earlier given or` (`the day` 28 `days after` `the day I asked`))
        ELSE JUST `the day I was notified`
    WHERE
        `the earlier given or` `the required day` MEANS
            IF `I have been given the statement`
            THEN `the earlier of` `the day I was given it` `and` `the required day`
            ELSE `the required day`

GIVEN `the enabling Act sets another period` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day I was notified` IS A DATE
      `I asked for reasons in time` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day I asked` IS A DATE
      `I have been given the statement` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day I was given it` IS A DATE
      `my s 159 application` IS A `My s 159 application`
      `the day set by the order` IS A DATE
      `the day I was told of the refusal` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`s 33(3) -- the last day to apply for review` `the enabling Act sets another period` `the day I was notified` `I asked for reasons in time` `the day I asked` `I have been given the statement` `the day I was given it` `my s 159 application` `the day set by the order` `the day I was told of the refusal` MEANS
    IF `the enabling Act sets another period`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE CONSIDER `s 33(4) -- the relevant day` `the day I was notified` `I asked for reasons in time` `the day I asked` `I have been given the statement` `the day I was given it` `my s 159 application` `the day set by the order` `the day I was told of the refusal`
         WHEN JUST `that day` THEN JUST (`the day` 28 `days after` `that day`)
         WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

@export By when must I apply to the Queensland Civil and Administrative Tribunal for review of a government decision, under the QCAT Act 2009 s 33(3), (4)? Within 28 days after the relevant day: the day you were notified of the decision, or, if you asked for reasons under s 158, the earlier of the day you got them and the day they were due (28 days after you asked), or the s 159 dates. The tribunal can extend the time (s 61). Answers nothing if another Act sets a different period, or while a s 159 application is undecided.
GIVEN `the enabling Act sets another period` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Act under which the decision was made sets its own period for applying to QCAT for review (if so, that period applies instead)
      `the day I was notified` IS A DATE @desc The day you were notified of the decision; ISO 8601
      `I asked for reasons in time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you asked the decision-maker in writing for a statement of reasons under s 158, within 14 days after being notified
      `the day I asked` IS A DATE @desc The day you asked for the statement of reasons (any date if you did not)
      `I have been given the statement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have been given the written statement of reasons you asked for
      `the day I was given it` IS A DATE @desc The day you were given it (any date if not)
      `my s 159 application` IS A `My s 159 application` @desc Whether you applied to the tribunal under s 159 for an order that the decision-maker give you the statement. One of: I have not applied for an order under s 159; the tribunal made the order; the tribunal refused to make the order; the tribunal has not yet decided
      `the day set by the order` IS A DATE @desc If the tribunal made a s 159 order: the day by which the order requires the statement to be given (any date otherwise)
      `the day I was told of the refusal` IS A DATE @desc If the tribunal refused the s 159 order: the day you were notified of that decision (any date otherwise)
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`By when must I apply for review` MEANS
    `s 33(3) -- the last day to apply for review` `the enabling Act sets another period` `the day I was notified` `I asked for reasons in time` `the day I asked` `I have been given the statement` `the day I was given it` `my s 159 application` `the day set by the order` `the day I was told of the refusal`

-- The rule: I DO file my application for review WITHIN the days from the relevant day to the last day. Not applying is
-- not a wrong; the BREACH marks only that the time passed. s 61(1)(a), (2): the tribunal may extend a time limit for
-- starting a proceeding "even if the time for complying ... has passed"; an extension is asked as a fact with its day.
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Party` `Act done`
`s 33(3) -- applying for review` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `I`
    DO `file my application for review`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    LEST BREACH BY `I` BECAUSE "the 28 days after the relevant day have passed without an application for review being filed"

@export Am I in time to apply to the Queensland Civil and Administrative Tribunal for review of a government decision, under the QCAT Act 2009 s 33(3), (4) and s 61? Answers nothing if another Act sets a different period, or while a s 159 application is undecided.
GIVEN `the enabling Act sets another period` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Act under which the decision was made sets its own period for applying to QCAT for review
      `the day I was notified` IS A DATE @desc The day you were notified of the decision; ISO 8601
      `I asked for reasons in time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you asked the decision-maker in writing for a statement of reasons under s 158, within 14 days after being notified
      `the day I asked` IS A DATE @desc The day you asked for the statement of reasons (any date if you did not)
      `I have been given the statement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have been given the written statement of reasons you asked for
      `the day I was given it` IS A DATE @desc The day you were given it (any date if not)
      `my s 159 application` IS A `My s 159 application` @desc Whether you applied to the tribunal under s 159 for an order that the decision-maker give you the statement. One of: I have not applied for an order under s 159; the tribunal made the order; the tribunal refused to make the order; the tribunal has not yet decided
      `the day set by the order` IS A DATE @desc If the tribunal made a s 159 order: the day by which the order requires the statement to be given (any date otherwise)
      `the day I was told of the refusal` IS A DATE @desc If the tribunal refused the s 159 order: the day you were notified of that decision (any date otherwise)
      `the day I file` IS A DATE @desc The day you file (or filed) the application for review in the registry; ISO 8601
      `the tribunal extended the time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the tribunal has ordered under s 61 that your time to apply be extended
      `the extended last day` IS A DATE @desc The last day under the extension (any date if there is none)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Am I in time to apply for review` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 33(4) -- the relevant day` `the day I was notified` `I asked for reasons in time` `the day I asked` `I have been given the statement` `the day I was given it` `my s 159 application` `the day set by the order` `the day I was told of the refusal`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
    WHEN JUST `the relevant day` THEN
        IF `the enabling Act sets another period`
        THEN NOTHING
        ELSE IF `the tribunal extended the time`
             THEN JUST (`the day` `the day I file` `is on or before` `the extended last day`)
             ELSE JUST (`done in time under` (`s 33(3) -- applying for review` 28) `file my application for review` `at` (`the days from` `the relevant day` `to` `the day I file`))

-- s 35(3) "The principal registrar may reject an application or referral on any of the following grounds-- (a) ... made
-- by a person who is not authorised to make it; (b) ... made after the expiry of the period within which it is required
-- to be made under this Act; (c) ... does not otherwise comply with this Act, an enabling Act or the rules." (4) on a
-- rejection or conditional acceptance the applicant may request a referral to the tribunal for review; (5) no fee.
-- (8) "The tribunal may direct the principal registrar to reject an application or referral only if a ground for
-- rejecting the application or referral under subsection (3) exists." With no ground the answer is "no" (fork F7).
-- s 33(2) "The application must-- (a) be in a form substantially complying with the rules; and (b) state the reasons for
-- the application; and (c) be filed in the registry." Its three limbs are the (c) ground here.
DECLARE `Can my application be rejected` IS ONE OF
    `no: there is no ground to reject it (s 35(3), (8))`
    `yes, it may be rejected: you are not authorised to make it (s 35(3)(a)); if it is rejected, you may ask without fee that the decision be referred to the tribunal for review (s 35(4), (5))`
    `yes, it may be rejected: it was made after the period for making it expired (s 35(3)(b)); if it is rejected, you may ask without fee that the decision be referred to the tribunal for review (s 35(4), (5)), and the tribunal may extend the time (s 61)`
    `yes, it may be rejected: it does not otherwise comply with the Act, the enabling Act or the rules, for example the form, the reasons or the filing (ss 33(2), 35(3)(c)); if it is rejected, you may ask without fee that the decision be referred to the tribunal for review (s 35(4), (5))`

@export Can my application to the Queensland Civil and Administrative Tribunal be rejected by the principal registrar, under the QCAT Act 2009 ss 33(2) and 35? Only if you are not authorised to make it, it is late, or it does not otherwise comply (it must be in the approved form, state your reasons and be filed in the registry). A rejection can be referred to the tribunal for review, free.
GIVEN `I am authorised to make it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a person the Act or the enabling Act lets make this application (for a minor civil dispute, the person who may apply; for a review, a person who may apply for review of the decision)
      `it is late` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was made after the period within which the Act requires it to be made (for a review, 28 days after the relevant day) and no extension has been given
      `it is in the approved form` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is in a form substantially complying with the QCAT rules
      `it states my reasons` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it states the reasons for the application
      `it otherwise complies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it otherwise complies with the Act, any enabling Act and the rules (it is filed in the registry, with what the rules require)
GIVETH A `Can my application be rejected`
`Can my application be rejected` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`I am authorised to make it`)
        THEN `yes, it may be rejected: you are not authorised to make it (s 35(3)(a)); if it is rejected, you may ask without fee that the decision be referred to the tribunal for review (s 35(4), (5))`
        IF `it is late`
        THEN `yes, it may be rejected: it was made after the period for making it expired (s 35(3)(b)); if it is rejected, you may ask without fee that the decision be referred to the tribunal for review (s 35(4), (5)), and the tribunal may extend the time (s 61)`
        IF NOT (`it is in the approved form` AND `it states my reasons` AND `it otherwise complies`)
        THEN `yes, it may be rejected: it does not otherwise comply with the Act, the enabling Act or the rules, for example the form, the reasons or the filing (ss 33(2), 35(3)(c)); if it is rejected, you may ask without fee that the decision be referred to the tribunal for review (s 35(4), (5))`
        OTHERWISE `no: there is no ground to reject it (s 35(3), (8))`

-- s 36 "A proceeding starts when the principal registrar accepts an application or referral, whether or not on
-- conditions." s 38(1) the applicant "must pay the prescribed fee, if any"; (2) "The tribunal must not take any action
-- on an application or referral until the fee is paid." The fee is set by regulation, not deposited: asked.
DECLARE `Has my proceeding started` IS ONE OF
    `not yet: a proceeding starts only when the principal registrar accepts the application (s 36)`
    `yes, it has started (s 36), but the tribunal must not take any action on it until you pay the prescribed fee (s 38)`
    `yes, it has started (s 36), and the tribunal may deal with it`

@export Has my proceeding in the Queensland Civil and Administrative Tribunal started, and can the tribunal act on it, under the QCAT Act 2009 ss 36 and 38? It starts when the principal registrar accepts the application; the tribunal takes no action until any prescribed fee is paid.
GIVEN `the registrar accepted it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the principal registrar has accepted your application (with or without conditions)
      `a fee is prescribed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a fee is prescribed by regulation for your kind of application
      `I have paid the fee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have paid that fee
GIVETH A `Has my proceeding started`
`Has my proceeding started` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`the registrar accepted it`)
        THEN `not yet: a proceeding starts only when the principal registrar accepts the application (s 36)`
        IF `a fee is prescribed` AND (NOT (`I have paid the fee`))
        THEN `yes, it has started (s 36), but the tribunal must not take any action on it until you pay the prescribed fee (s 38)`
        OTHERWISE `yes, it has started (s 36), and the tribunal may deal with it`

-- s 37(2) "The applicant ... must, within the period stated in the rules, give a copy of the application or referral to--
-- (a) each party to the proceeding; and (b) each other person to whom notice ... must be given under an enabling Act or
-- the rules; and (c) any person the tribunal directs". (3) "Subsection (2) does not require the applicant to give a copy
-- ... if-- (a) the principal registrar has given or undertaken to give the copy to the person; or (b) under subsection
-- (4), the tribunal makes an order that the copy is not required ...; or (c) the rules exempt the applicant". The period
-- is in the rules (not deposited).
@export Must I give a copy of my application to the other party (and anyone else entitled to notice), under the QCAT Act 2009 s 37? Yes, within the period in the QCAT rules, unless the principal registrar has given or undertaken to give it, the tribunal has exempted you, or the rules exempt you.
GIVEN `the registrar gave or undertook to give it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the principal registrar has given, or undertaken to give, the copy to that person
      `the tribunal exempted me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the tribunal has ordered under s 37(4) that you need not give the copy to that person (for example because you could not find them after all reasonable attempts)
      `the rules exempt me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the QCAT rules exempt you from giving the copy to that person
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I give a copy of my application to the other party` IF
    NOT (`the registrar gave or undertook to give it` OR `the tribunal exempted me` OR `the rules exempt me`)

-- s 61(1) the tribunal may "(a) extend a time limit fixed for the start of a proceeding by this Act or an enabling Act;
-- or (b) extend or shorten a time limit fixed by this Act, an enabling Act or the rules; or (c) waive compliance with
-- another procedural requirement". (2) "even if the time for complying with the relevant requirement has passed". (3)
-- "The tribunal can not extend or shorten a time limit ... if to do so would cause prejudice or detriment, not able to
-- be remedied by an appropriate order for costs or damages, to a party or potential party". A flat negative: JUST FALSE.
-- Otherwise "may": JUST TRUE means only that the tribunal has the power.
@export Can the Queensland Civil and Administrative Tribunal extend my time to apply, appeal or do another step, even after the time has passed, under the QCAT Act 2009 s 61? It can, unless the extension would cause another party prejudice that an order for costs or damages could not put right. Whether to extend is the tribunal's choice.
GIVEN `it would cause prejudice that costs or damages cannot remedy` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether extending the time would cause another party or potential party prejudice or detriment that could not be remedied by an order for costs or damages (in the end the tribunal's view)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the tribunal extend my time` MEANS
    IF `it would cause prejudice that costs or damages cannot remedy`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE JUST TRUE

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `s 43 -- representation`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 43(1) "The main purpose of this section is to have parties represent themselves unless the interests of justice
-- require otherwise." (2) "a party-- (a) may appear without representation; or (b) may be represented by someone else
-- if-- (i) the party is a child or a person with impaired capacity; or (ii) the proceeding relates to taking disciplinary
-- action, or reviewing a decision about taking disciplinary action, against a person; or (iii) an enabling Act that is an
-- Act, or the rules, states the person may be represented; or (iv) the party has been given leave by the tribunal".
-- "if": JUST FALSE kind when none holds. (3) circumstances supporting leave. (4) "A party can not be represented ... by a
-- person-- (a) who, under rules made under section 224(3), is disqualified ...; or (b) who is not an Australian legal
-- practitioner or government legal officer, unless the tribunal is satisfied the person is an appropriate person". A
-- flat negative. "impaired capacity" is borrowed from the Guardianship and Administration Act 2000: asked. "child" is an
-- individual under 18 (schedule 3): worked out from age.
DECLARE `Can I be represented` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you may be represented by that person (s 43(2)(b)); you may always appear for yourself instead (s 43(2)(a))`
    `no, not without the tribunal's leave: you must appear for yourself unless you are a child or a person with impaired capacity, the proceeding is about disciplinary action, an Act or the rules allow representation, or the tribunal gives leave (s 43(1), (2)); the tribunal may weigh the complexity of the case, whether another party is represented and whether all parties agree (s 43(3))`
    `no: that person is disqualified under the rules from representing a party (s 43(4)(a))`
    `no: that person is not an Australian legal practitioner or government legal officer, and the tribunal is not satisfied they are an appropriate person to represent you (s 43(4)(b))`

@export Can I have a lawyer or someone else represent me in the Queensland Civil and Administrative Tribunal, under the QCAT Act 2009 s 43? Parties are meant to represent themselves; representation is allowed for children and people with impaired capacity, in disciplinary matters, where an Act or the QCAT rules allow it, or with the tribunal's leave.
GIVEN `my age` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age in whole years
      `I have impaired capacity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a person with impaired capacity (within the meaning of the Guardianship and Administration Act 2000)
      `it is about disciplinary action` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proceeding relates to taking disciplinary action against a person, or reviewing a decision about taking such action
      `an Act or the rules allow it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Act that gives QCAT jurisdiction over your matter, or the QCAT rules, says a party may be represented
      `the tribunal gave me leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the tribunal has given you leave to be represented
      `the person is disqualified` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person you want is disqualified under the QCAT rules from representing a party
      `the person is a lawyer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person you want is an Australian legal practitioner or a government legal officer
      `the tribunal accepts the person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the person is not a lawyer: whether the tribunal is satisfied they are an appropriate person to represent you
GIVETH A `Can I be represented`
`Can I be represented` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (    (`my age` LESS THAN 18)
                 OR `I have impaired capacity`
                 OR `it is about disciplinary action`
                 OR `an Act or the rules allow it`
                 OR `the tribunal gave me leave`)
        THEN `no, not without the tribunal's leave: you must appear for yourself unless you are a child or a person with impaired capacity, the proceeding is about disciplinary action, an Act or the rules allow representation, or the tribunal gives leave (s 43(1), (2)); the tribunal may weigh the complexity of the case, whether another party is represented and whether all parties agree (s 43(3))`
        IF `the person is disqualified`
        THEN `no: that person is disqualified under the rules from representing a party (s 43(4)(a))`
        IF NOT (`the person is a lawyer` OR `the tribunal accepts the person`)
        THEN `no: that person is not an Australian legal practitioner or government legal officer, and the tribunal is not satisfied they are an appropriate person to represent you (s 43(4)(b))`
        OTHERWISE `yes: you may be represented by that person (s 43(2)(b)); you may always appear for yourself instead (s 43(2)(a))`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 46, 49 -- withdrawing, and starting again`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 46(1) "An applicant may, in the way stated in the rules, withdraw the applicant's application ... before the matter
-- is heard and decided by the tribunal." (2) "However, if the application ... is made under any of the following, the
-- applicant may withdraw ... only with the leave of the tribunal-- (a) the Child Protection Act 1999; (b) the Disability
-- Services Act 2006, section 178(9); (c) the Guardianship and Administration Act 2000; (d) the Powers of Attorney Act
-- 1998." After the matter is heard and decided: s 46 gives no right (a period in the text's words: JUST FALSE kind).
DECLARE `Can I withdraw` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you may withdraw it, in the way stated in the rules (s 46(1)); you then need the tribunal's leave to bring a further application on the same facts (s 46(3))`
    `only with the tribunal's leave: applications under the Child Protection Act 1999, the Disability Services Act 2006 s 178(9), the Guardianship and Administration Act 2000 and the Powers of Attorney Act 1998 can be withdrawn only with leave (s 46(2))`
    `no: an application can be withdrawn only before the matter is heard and decided (s 46(1))`

@export Can I withdraw my application to the Queensland Civil and Administrative Tribunal, under the QCAT Act 2009 s 46? Yes, before it is heard and decided (with the tribunal's leave for child protection, disability services s 178(9), guardianship and powers of attorney applications). Withdrawing means you need leave to bring the same claim again.
GIVEN `it has been heard and decided` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the tribunal has already heard and decided the matter
      `it is under a listed Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the application is made under the Child Protection Act 1999, the Disability Services Act 2006 section 178(9), the Guardianship and Administration Act 2000 or the Powers of Attorney Act 1998
GIVETH A `Can I withdraw`
`Can I withdraw my application` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `it has been heard and decided`
        THEN `no: an application can be withdrawn only before the matter is heard and decided (s 46(1))`
        IF `it is under a listed Act`
        THEN `only with the tribunal's leave: applications under the Child Protection Act 1999, the Disability Services Act 2006 s 178(9), the Guardianship and Administration Act 2000 and the Powers of Attorney Act 1998 can be withdrawn only with leave (s 46(2))`
        OTHERWISE `yes: you may withdraw it, in the way stated in the rules (s 46(1)); you then need the tribunal's leave to bring a further application on the same facts (s 46(3))`

-- s 46(3) "If an applicant withdraws an application ..., the applicant can not make a further application ... relating to
-- the same facts or circumstances without leave of the tribunal." s 49(1)-(2): after a dismissal or striking out under
-- s 47 (frivolous, vexatious, misconceived, lacking in substance, abuse of process) or s 48 (causing disadvantage),
-- "Another proceeding ... of the same kind relating to the same matter can not be started before the tribunal without the
-- leave of the president or deputy president." (3) leave "if ... the interests of justice requires it"; (4) may extend
-- any time limit; (5) reinstatement if dismissed in error. Flat negatives: JUST FALSE kind without leave. Any other end
-- of the earlier proceeding is outside these sections: "not covered".
DECLARE `How my earlier application ended` IS ONE OF
    `I withdrew it`
    `it was dismissed or struck out under s 47 or s 48`
    `it ended another way`

DECLARE `Can I start again` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the tribunal gave you leave to make a further application on the same facts (s 46(3))`
    `no, not without the tribunal's leave: after withdrawing you need leave to make a further application relating to the same facts or circumstances (s 46(3))`
    `yes: the president or deputy president gave you leave to start another proceeding of the same kind on the same matter, and may have extended the time limit (s 49(2)-(4))`
    `no, not without the leave of the president or deputy president, given if the interests of justice require it (s 49(2), (3)); if the dismissal was an error, you may ask the tribunal to reinstate the proceeding (s 49(5), (6))`
    `not covered: ss 46(3) and 49 deal only with a withdrawn application and a proceeding dismissed or struck out under s 47 or s 48`

@export Can I bring the same claim to the Queensland Civil and Administrative Tribunal again after I withdrew it, or after it was dismissed or struck out, under the QCAT Act 2009 ss 46(3) and 49? After withdrawing you need the tribunal's leave; after a dismissal or strike-out for being unjustified (s 47) or for causing disadvantage (s 48) you need the leave of the president or deputy president.
GIVEN `how it ended` IS A `How my earlier application ended` @desc How your earlier application or proceeding ended. One of: I withdrew it; it was dismissed or struck out under s 47 or s 48; it ended another way
      `I have leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have been given the leave needed (the tribunal's leave after a withdrawal; the president's or deputy president's leave after a dismissal or strike-out)
GIVETH A `Can I start again`
`Can I bring the same claim again` MEANS
    CONSIDER `how it ended`
    WHEN `I withdrew it` THEN
        IF `I have leave`
        THEN `yes: the tribunal gave you leave to make a further application on the same facts (s 46(3))`
        ELSE `no, not without the tribunal's leave: after withdrawing you need leave to make a further application relating to the same facts or circumstances (s 46(3))`
    WHEN `it was dismissed or struck out under s 47 or s 48` THEN
        IF `I have leave`
        THEN `yes: the president or deputy president gave you leave to start another proceeding of the same kind on the same matter, and may have extended the time limit (s 49(2)-(4))`
        ELSE `no, not without the leave of the president or deputy president, given if the interests of justice require it (s 49(2), (3)); if the dismissal was an error, you may ask the tribunal to reinstate the proceeding (s 49(5), (6))`
    WHEN `it ended another way` THEN `not covered: ss 46(3) and 49 deal only with a withdrawn application and a proceeding dismissed or struck out under s 47 or s 48`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 50, 50A, 51, 51A -- decision by default` -- arises only if the respondent has not responded to your application in time
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 50(1) "This section applies if-- (a) a person has applied to the tribunal to recover a debt or liquidated demand of
-- money from a person (the respondent); and (b) an enabling Act that is an Act or the rules state that the respondent
-- must respond to the application within a stated period; and (c) the respondent has not responded to the application
-- within the stated period." (2) "The applicant may ... apply ... for a decision by default for an amount limited to--
-- (a) the amount claimed ...; and (b) interest on the amount claimed at the rate the tribunal considers appropriate; and
-- (c) either-- (i) for an application for a minor civil dispute--costs stated in the rules as costs that may be awarded
-- for minor civil disputes under section 102; or (ii) [otherwise] (A) the fee paid for the application; and (B) legal
-- costs based on a scale stated in the rules." (3) the principal registrar may make it; (4) it is a final decision; (5)
-- "The applicant must prove the respondent has been given a copy of the application before a decision by default may be
-- made". s 50A: the same for unliquidated damages, "conditional on the assessment by the tribunal of the unliquidated
-- damages", which the tribunal must then assess (s 50A(5)(b)). "applies if": JUST FALSE kind when a limb fails.
DECLARE `What I am claiming` IS ONE OF
    `a debt or liquidated demand of money`
    `an amount consisting of or including unliquidated damages`
    `something else`

DECLARE `Can I get a decision by default` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you may apply for a decision by default for no more than the amount claimed, interest at the rate the tribunal considers appropriate, and the costs the rules allow for minor civil disputes; the principal registrar may make it, and it is a final decision (s 50(2)(a), (b), (c)(i), (3), (4))`
    `yes: you may apply for a decision by default for no more than the amount claimed, interest at the rate the tribunal considers appropriate, the application fee and legal costs on the rules' scale; the principal registrar may make it, and it is a final decision (s 50(2)(a), (b), (c)(ii), (3), (4))`
    `yes: you may apply for a decision by default conditional on the tribunal assessing the unliquidated damages, also claiming any liquidated amount, interest, the application fee and scale costs; if given, it is a final decision and the tribunal must assess the damages (s 50A(2)-(5))`
    `no: a decision by default is only for a claim for a debt, a liquidated demand of money or unliquidated damages (ss 50(1)(a), 50A(1)(a))`
    `no: no Act or rule requires the respondent to respond within a stated period (ss 50(1)(b), 50A(1)(b))`
    `no: the respondent responded within the stated period (ss 50(1)(c), 50A(1)(c))`
    `not yet: you must first prove the respondent has been given a copy of the application (ss 50(5), 50A(6))`

@export Can I get a decision by default in the Queensland Civil and Administrative Tribunal because the respondent did not respond to my claim in time, under the QCAT Act 2009 ss 50 and 50A? It is available for debts, fixed amounts of money and unliquidated damages, once the time to respond has passed and you can prove the respondent was given a copy of the application. The respondent can later apply to set it aside (s 51).
GIVEN `what I am claiming` IS A `What I am claiming` @desc What your application claims. One of: a debt or liquidated demand of money; an amount consisting of or including unliquidated damages (damages the tribunal must assess); something else
      `a period to respond is set` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an Act or the QCAT rules say the respondent must respond to your application within a stated period
      `the respondent did not respond in time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the respondent has not responded within that period
      `I can prove they were given a copy` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you can prove the respondent has been given a copy of your application
      `it is a minor civil dispute` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your application is for a minor civil dispute
GIVETH A `Can I get a decision by default`
`Can I get a decision by default` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `what I am claiming` EQUALS `something else`
        THEN `no: a decision by default is only for a claim for a debt, a liquidated demand of money or unliquidated damages (ss 50(1)(a), 50A(1)(a))`
        IF NOT (`a period to respond is set`)
        THEN `no: no Act or rule requires the respondent to respond within a stated period (ss 50(1)(b), 50A(1)(b))`
        IF NOT (`the respondent did not respond in time`)
        THEN `no: the respondent responded within the stated period (ss 50(1)(c), 50A(1)(c))`
        IF NOT (`I can prove they were given a copy`)
        THEN `not yet: you must first prove the respondent has been given a copy of the application (ss 50(5), 50A(6))`
        IF `what I am claiming` EQUALS `an amount consisting of or including unliquidated damages`
        THEN `yes: you may apply for a decision by default conditional on the tribunal assessing the unliquidated damages, also claiming any liquidated amount, interest, the application fee and scale costs; if given, it is a final decision and the tribunal must assess the damages (s 50A(2)-(5))`
        IF `it is a minor civil dispute`
        THEN `yes: you may apply for a decision by default for no more than the amount claimed, interest at the rate the tribunal considers appropriate, and the costs the rules allow for minor civil disputes; the principal registrar may make it, and it is a final decision (s 50(2)(a), (b), (c)(i), (3), (4))`
        OTHERWISE `yes: you may apply for a decision by default for no more than the amount claimed, interest at the rate the tribunal considers appropriate, the application fee and legal costs on the rules' scale; the principal registrar may make it, and it is a final decision (s 50(2)(a), (b), (c)(ii), (3), (4))`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 84-89 -- settlement` -- arises only if you and the other party settle, or an accepted offer is not kept
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 84(2) at a compulsory conference the person presiding may record the terms and make orders giving effect. s 85(2) a
-- conciliator or mediator who is a member, adjudicator or the principal registrar may do the same; (4)-(5) another
-- conciliator or mediator may have the parties sign the terms and file them, and the tribunal may then make orders. s 86
-- (elsewhere): "(2) The parties may-- (a) record the terms of the settlement in writing and sign the written terms; and
-- (b) file the signed written terms in the registry. (3) If no party to the proceeding notifies the tribunal of the
-- party's intention to withdraw from the settlement within 7 days after the written terms are filed in the registry, the
-- tribunal may make the orders necessary to give effect to the settlement." s 87: only "if the entity making the order is
-- satisfied the tribunal could make a decision in the terms of the settlement or in terms consistent with" it. s 88(1)
-- the order has the effect of a decision after deciding the proceeding. "if" and "only if": JUST FALSE kind. A notice of
-- withdrawal on the 7th day after filing is within the 7 days (fork F8).
DECLARE `Where we settled` IS ONE OF
    `in a compulsory conference`
    `at conciliation or mediation conducted by a member, an adjudicator or the principal registrar`
    `at conciliation or mediation conducted by someone else`
    `elsewhere`

DECLARE `Can our settlement be made an order` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the person presiding at the conference, or the conciliator or mediator, may record the terms and make the orders needed to give effect to the settlement, which have the effect of a tribunal decision (ss 84(2), 85(2), 88(1))`
    `yes: the signed terms have been filed, so the tribunal may make the orders needed to give effect to the settlement, which have the effect of a tribunal decision (ss 85(5), 88(1))`
    `yes: no party gave notice of withdrawing from the settlement within 7 days after the signed terms were filed, so the tribunal may make the orders needed to give effect to it, which have the effect of a tribunal decision (ss 86(3), 88(1))`
    `no: the signed written terms of the settlement have not been filed in the registry (ss 85(4), (5), 86(2), (3))`
    `no: a party gave notice of withdrawing from the settlement within 7 days after the signed terms were filed (s 86(3))`
    `no: an order giving effect to a settlement may be made only if the tribunal could make a decision in those terms or terms consistent with them (s 87)`

@export Can the settlement I reached with the other party be made an order of the Queensland Civil and Administrative Tribunal, under the QCAT Act 2009 ss 84-88? At a compulsory conference, or at conciliation or mediation run by a tribunal officer, the officer can make the orders; otherwise the signed terms must be filed, and for a settlement reached elsewhere no party may give notice of withdrawing within 7 days after filing. The tribunal must be able to make a decision in those terms.
GIVEN `where we settled` IS A `Where we settled` @desc Where the settlement was reached. One of: in a compulsory conference; at conciliation or mediation conducted by a member, an adjudicator or the principal registrar; at conciliation or mediation conducted by someone else; elsewhere
      `the signed terms were filed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the written terms of the settlement, signed by the parties, have been filed in the QCAT registry
      `the day they were filed` IS A DATE @desc The day the signed terms were filed (any date if not filed); ISO 8601
      `a party gave notice of withdrawing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a settlement reached elsewhere: whether a party has notified the tribunal of an intention to withdraw from the settlement
      `the day of that notice` IS A DATE @desc The day that notice was given (any date if none)
      `the tribunal could decide in those terms` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the tribunal could make a decision in the terms of the settlement or consistent with them (in the end the view of whoever makes the order)
GIVETH A `Can our settlement be made an order`
`Can our settlement be made an order` MEANS
    IF NOT (`the tribunal could decide in those terms`)
    THEN `no: an order giving effect to a settlement may be made only if the tribunal could make a decision in those terms or terms consistent with them (s 87)`
    ELSE CONSIDER `where we settled`
         WHEN `in a compulsory conference` THEN `yes: the person presiding at the conference, or the conciliator or mediator, may record the terms and make the orders needed to give effect to the settlement, which have the effect of a tribunal decision (ss 84(2), 85(2), 88(1))`
         WHEN `at conciliation or mediation conducted by a member, an adjudicator or the principal registrar` THEN `yes: the person presiding at the conference, or the conciliator or mediator, may record the terms and make the orders needed to give effect to the settlement, which have the effect of a tribunal decision (ss 84(2), 85(2), 88(1))`
         WHEN `at conciliation or mediation conducted by someone else` THEN
            IF `the signed terms were filed`
            THEN `yes: the signed terms have been filed, so the tribunal may make the orders needed to give effect to the settlement, which have the effect of a tribunal decision (ss 85(5), 88(1))`
            ELSE `no: the signed written terms of the settlement have not been filed in the registry (ss 85(4), (5), 86(2), (3))`
         WHEN `elsewhere` THEN
            BRANCH
                IF NOT (`the signed terms were filed`)
                THEN `no: the signed written terms of the settlement have not been filed in the registry (ss 85(4), (5), 86(2), (3))`
                IF `a party gave notice of withdrawing` AND (`the day` `the day of that notice` `is on or before` (`the day` 7 `days after` `the day they were filed`))
                THEN `no: a party gave notice of withdrawing from the settlement within 7 days after the signed terms were filed (s 86(3))`
                OTHERWISE `yes: no party gave notice of withdrawing from the settlement within 7 days after the signed terms were filed, so the tribunal may make the orders needed to give effect to it, which have the effect of a tribunal decision (ss 86(3), 88(1))`

@export For a settlement reached outside a compulsory conference, conciliation or mediation, what is the last day a party can give the Queensland Civil and Administrative Tribunal notice of withdrawing from it, under the QCAT Act 2009 s 86(3)? The 7th day after the signed terms are filed in the registry.
GIVEN `the day they were filed` IS A DATE @desc The day the signed written terms of the settlement were filed in the QCAT registry; ISO 8601
GIVETH A DATE
`By when can a party withdraw from our settlement` MEANS
    `the day` 7 `days after` `the day they were filed`

-- s 89(1) "This section applies if an offer to settle the dispute ... is accepted, but the party who made the offer does
-- not comply with its terms." (2) "The tribunal, on the application of the party who accepted the offer (the relevant
-- party), may-- (a) make an order giving effect to the terms of the offer; or (b) if the party making the offer was the
-- applicant-- (i) dismiss the proceeding; or (ii) if the relevant party responded to the application ... before the offer
-- was made, make an order awarding the relevant party any or all of the things asked for in the response; or (c) if the
-- relevant party is the applicant, make an order awarding the relevant party any or all of the things asked for in the
-- proceeding." "applies if": "no" when the offer was not accepted by me or was complied with.
DECLARE `What I can ask for on a broken settlement offer` IS ONE OF
    `you may apply for an order giving effect to the terms of the offer, or an order awarding you any or all of the things you asked for in the proceeding (s 89(2)(a), (c))`
    `you may apply for an order giving effect to the terms of the offer, dismissal of the proceeding, or an order awarding you any or all of the things asked for in your response (s 89(2)(a), (b))`
    `you may apply for an order giving effect to the terms of the offer, or dismissal of the proceeding (s 89(2)(a), (b)(i))`
    `nothing under s 89: it applies only where you accepted an offer to settle and the party who made it has not complied with its terms (s 89(1), (2))`

@export The other party made an offer to settle, I accepted it, and they have not kept to it: what can I ask the Queensland Civil and Administrative Tribunal for, under the QCAT Act 2009 s 89? If you are the applicant: an order giving effect to the offer, or an order giving you what you asked for in the proceeding.
GIVEN `I accepted the offer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you accepted an offer to settle the dispute made by the other party
      `they have not complied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the party who made the offer has not complied with its terms
      `I am the applicant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the applicant in the proceeding (answer no if you are the respondent and the applicant made the offer)
      `I responded before the offer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you are the respondent: whether you responded to the application before the offer was made
GIVETH A `What I can ask for on a broken settlement offer`
`What can I ask for if an accepted offer is not kept` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`I accepted the offer` AND `they have not complied`)
        THEN `nothing under s 89: it applies only where you accepted an offer to settle and the party who made it has not complied with its terms (s 89(1), (2))`
        IF `I am the applicant`
        THEN `you may apply for an order giving effect to the terms of the offer, or an order awarding you any or all of the things you asked for in the proceeding (s 89(2)(a), (c))`
        IF `I responded before the offer`
        THEN `you may apply for an order giving effect to the terms of the offer, dismissal of the proceeding, or an order awarding you any or all of the things asked for in your response (s 89(2)(a), (b))`
        OTHERWISE `you may apply for an order giving effect to the terms of the offer, or dismissal of the proceeding (s 89(2)(a), (b)(i))`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 100-103, 109 -- costs`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 100 "Other than as provided under this Act or an enabling Act, each party to a proceeding must bear the party's own
-- costs for the proceeding." s 101(1) "The tribunal must not award costs against a child." (2) a representative of a
-- child may still be ordered to pay under s 103. s 102(1) the tribunal "may make an order requiring a party ... to pay
-- all or a stated part of the costs of another party ... if the tribunal considers the interests of justice require
-- it". (2) "However, the only costs the tribunal may award ... against a party to a proceeding for a minor civil dispute
-- are the costs stated in the rules as costs that may be awarded for minor civil disputes under this section." (3) the
-- factors (disadvantaging another party as in s 48(1), complexity, relative strength of claims, for a review the
-- decision-maker's natural justice and the applicant's help, financial circumstances, anything else). s 47(2)(c) and
-- s 48(2)(c) costs orders on an unjustified proceeding or a party causing disadvantage are further routes, folded into
-- the "interests of justice" input (fork F9). Where an enabling Act provides for costs: "not covered" by this Act alone.
DECLARE `Can I be made to pay costs` IS ONE OF
    `no: the tribunal must not award costs against a child (s 101(1)); a representative of yours may still be ordered to pay costs they caused (ss 101(2), 103)`
    `no: each party bears their own costs (s 100), unless the tribunal considers the interests of justice require a costs order (s 102(1))`
    `yes, if the tribunal considers the interests of justice require it, but in a minor civil dispute only the costs the rules allow for minor civil disputes (s 102(1), (2))`
    `yes, if the tribunal considers the interests of justice require it: all or a stated part of the other party's costs (s 102(1)), fixed by the tribunal where possible or assessed under the rules (s 107)`
    `not covered: the enabling Act provides for costs in this proceeding, and this encoding reads this Act alone (s 100)`

@export Can I be ordered to pay the other party's legal costs in the Queensland Civil and Administrative Tribunal, under the QCAT Act 2009 ss 100-102? Usually each party pays their own costs; the tribunal can order costs where the interests of justice require it (in a minor civil dispute only the limited costs the rules allow), and never against a child.
GIVEN `my age` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age in whole years
      `an enabling Act provides for costs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Act that gives QCAT jurisdiction over your matter has its own provisions about costs
      `it is a minor civil dispute` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your proceeding is for a minor civil dispute
      `the interests of justice require it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the tribunal considers the interests of justice require you to pay costs (it may weigh whether you unnecessarily disadvantaged the other party, the complexity of the dispute, the strength of each side's claims, and the parties' financial circumstances)
GIVETH A `Can I be made to pay costs`
`Can I be ordered to pay the other party's costs` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `my age` LESS THAN 18
        THEN `no: the tribunal must not award costs against a child (s 101(1)); a representative of yours may still be ordered to pay costs they caused (ss 101(2), 103)`
        IF `an enabling Act provides for costs`
        THEN `not covered: the enabling Act provides for costs in this proceeding, and this encoding reads this Act alone (s 100)`
        IF NOT (`the interests of justice require it`)
        THEN `no: each party bears their own costs (s 100), unless the tribunal considers the interests of justice require a costs order (s 102(1))`
        IF `it is a minor civil dispute`
        THEN `yes, if the tribunal considers the interests of justice require it, but in a minor civil dispute only the costs the rules allow for minor civil disputes (s 102(1), (2))`
        OTHERWISE `yes, if the tribunal considers the interests of justice require it: all or a stated part of the other party's costs (s 102(1)), fixed by the tribunal where possible or assessed under the rules (s 107)`

-- s 109(1) "This section applies if, under this Act or an enabling Act, the tribunal may award a party's costs for a
-- proceeding." (2) "On the application of a party (applicant party) to the proceeding against whom a claim is made or an
-- outcome or decision sought ..., the tribunal may make an order-- (a) requiring another party to the proceeding to give
-- security for the applicant party's costs within the period stated in the order; and (b) staying the proceeding ...
-- until the security is given." (3) if not given in time, the tribunal "may make an order dismissing the proceeding". For
-- a child, against whom costs cannot be awarded (s 101), whether s 109 can still reach them is not said (finding 4, fork F14):
-- "not covered".
DECLARE `Can I be made to give security for costs` IS ONE OF
    `yes: on the other party's application, the tribunal may order you to give security for their costs within a stated period and stay the proceeding until you do; if you do not, it may dismiss the proceeding (s 109(2), (3)); it may weigh your finances and the merits and genuineness of your claim (s 109(4))`
    `no: only a party against whom a claim is made, or an outcome or decision sought, may apply for security (s 109(2))`
    `not covered: costs cannot be awarded against a child (s 101(1)), and s 109 does not say whether security can still be ordered`

@export Can I be ordered to give security for the other party's costs before my claim in the Queensland Civil and Administrative Tribunal goes ahead, under the QCAT Act 2009 s 109? The party you claim against can apply; the tribunal may order security, pause the proceeding until it is given, and dismiss it if it is not.
GIVEN `my age` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age in whole years
      `the other party is one I claim against` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the party seeking security is a party against whom you make a claim or seek an outcome or decision
GIVETH A `Can I be made to give security for costs`
`Can I be ordered to give security for costs` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `my age` LESS THAN 18
        THEN `not covered: costs cannot be awarded against a child (s 101(1)), and s 109 does not say whether security can still be ordered`
        IF NOT (`the other party is one I claim against`)
        THEN `no: only a party against whom a claim is made, or an outcome or decision sought, may apply for security (s 109(2))`
        OTHERWISE `yes: on the other party's application, the tribunal may order you to give security for their costs within a stated period and stay the proceeding until you do; if you do not, it may dismiss the proceeding (s 109(2), (3)); it may weigh your finances and the merits and genuineness of your claim (s 109(4))`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 122, 126, 127 -- the decision and its reasons`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 127 "A decision of the tribunal in a proceeding takes effect-- (a) when it is made; or (b) if the decision states a
-- later date or time when the decision is to take effect--the later date or time."
GIVEN `the day it was made` IS A DATE
      `it states a later day` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the later day` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`s 127 -- the day the decision takes effect` `the day it was made` `it states a later day` `the later day` MEANS
    IF `it states a later day`
    THEN `the later day`
    ELSE `the day it was made`

-- s 122(1) "This section applies if the tribunal makes a decision ... and does not give written reasons for the
-- decision." (2) "A party ... may, within 14 days after the decision takes effect under section 127, request that the
-- tribunal give written reasons". (3) "The tribunal must comply with a request ... within 45 days after the request is
-- made or, if the president extends the period, the extended period." (4) "However, the tribunal is not required to
-- comply with a request for written reasons for a decision made under section 51, 54(1), 55(1), 56(1), 57, 61(1), 62(1)
-- or (3), 63(1) or (4) or 64(1)." "applies if" and a period fixed in the text: JUST FALSE kind.
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Party` `Act done`
`s 122(2) -- asking the tribunal for written reasons` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `I`
    DO `ask the tribunal for written reasons`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    LEST BREACH BY `I` BECAUSE "the 14 days after the decision took effect have passed; the request under section 122 is out of time"

DECLARE `Can I ask for written reasons` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the tribunal must give you written reasons within 45 days after your request, or a longer period the president allows (s 122(2), (3))`
    `yes, you may ask, but for this kind of procedural decision the tribunal is not required to give written reasons (s 122(4))`
    `no: the tribunal has already given written reasons for the decision (s 122(1))`
    `no: a request must be made within 14 days after the decision takes effect (ss 122(2), 127)`

@export Can I ask the Queensland Civil and Administrative Tribunal for written reasons for its decision, under the QCAT Act 2009 ss 122 and 127? You may ask within 14 days after the decision takes effect, and the tribunal must give them within 45 days, except for some procedural decisions. Written reasons matter: the time to appeal often runs from the day you get them (ss 143, 151).
GIVEN `I have written reasons` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the tribunal has already given written reasons for the decision (a written transcript or audio recording of oral reasons counts, s 123)
      `the day it was made` IS A DATE @desc The day the decision was made; ISO 8601
      `it states a later day` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision states a later day on which it takes effect
      `the later day` IS A DATE @desc That later day (any date if none)
      `the day I ask` IS A DATE @desc The day you make (or made) the request; ISO 8601
      `it is a listed procedural decision` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision was made under s 51 (setting aside a decision by default), 54(1), 55(1), 56(1) (consolidating or ordering proceedings), 57 (general powers), 61(1) (extending time), 62(1) or (3) (directions), 63(1) or (4) (documents from others) or 64(1) (amending documents)
GIVETH A `Can I ask for written reasons`
`Can I ask the tribunal for written reasons` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `I have written reasons`
        THEN `no: the tribunal has already given written reasons for the decision (s 122(1))`
        IF NOT (`done in time under` (`s 122(2) -- asking the tribunal for written reasons` 14) `ask the tribunal for written reasons` `at` (`the days from` (`s 127 -- the day the decision takes effect` `the day it was made` `it states a later day` `the later day`) `to` `the day I ask`))
        THEN `no: a request must be made within 14 days after the decision takes effect (ss 122(2), 127)`
        IF `it is a listed procedural decision`
        THEN `yes, you may ask, but for this kind of procedural decision the tribunal is not required to give written reasons (s 122(4))`
        OTHERWISE `yes: the tribunal must give you written reasons within 45 days after your request, or a longer period the president allows (s 122(2), (3))`

@export By when must I ask the Queensland Civil and Administrative Tribunal for written reasons for its decision, under the QCAT Act 2009 ss 122(2) and 127? Within 14 days after the decision takes effect (the day it is made, or a later day it states).
GIVEN `the day it was made` IS A DATE @desc The day the decision was made; ISO 8601
      `it states a later day` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision states a later day on which it takes effect
      `the later day` IS A DATE @desc That later day (any date if none)
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I ask the tribunal for written reasons` MEANS
    `the day` 14 `days after` (`s 127 -- the day the decision takes effect` `the day it was made` `it states a later day` `the later day`)

@export By when must the Queensland Civil and Administrative Tribunal give me the written reasons I asked for, under the QCAT Act 2009 s 122(3)? Within 45 days after your request, unless the president extends the period.
GIVEN `the day I asked` IS A DATE @desc The day you asked the tribunal for written reasons; ISO 8601
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must the tribunal give me written reasons` MEANS
    `the day` 45 `days after` `the day I asked`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 129-134 -- enforcing and renewing the decision` -- arises only if the tribunal decided in your favour and it has not been complied with
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 129: "final decision ... includes-- (a) an interim order under section 58; and (b) an injunction under section 59;
-- and (c) a monetary decision made other than as part of the tribunal's final decision". s 131(2) "A person may enforce
-- the final decision [requiring payment] by filing a copy of the decision in the registry of a court of competent
-- jurisdiction." (3) it is "taken to be a money order of the court". s 132(2), (3) other final decisions: filed in "the
-- relevant court", taken to be a non-money order; (6) "relevant court means-- (a) for a final decision ... relating to a
-- minor civil dispute--a Magistrates Court; or (b) for another final decision ...--the Supreme Court." A decision that
-- is not a final decision as so defined: "includes" is open, so "not covered".
DECLARE `Kind of decision to enforce` IS ONE OF
    `the final decision in the proceeding`
    `an interim order under s 58`
    `an injunction under s 59`
    `a monetary decision that is not part of the final decision`
    `another decision`

DECLARE `How I enforce it` IS ONE OF
    `file a copy of the decision in the registry of a court of competent jurisdiction; it is then a money order of that court and is enforced as one (s 131)`
    `file a copy of the decision in the registry of a Magistrates Court; it is then a non-money order of that court and is enforced as one (s 132(2), (3), (6)(a))`
    `file a copy of the decision in the registry of the Supreme Court; it is then a non-money order of that court and is enforced as one (s 132(2), (3), (6)(b))`
    `not covered: part 7, division 4 enforces final decisions, interim orders, injunctions and monetary decisions (s 129)`

@export How do I enforce a decision of the Queensland Civil and Administrative Tribunal that the other party has not complied with, under the QCAT Act 2009 ss 129-132? A decision to pay you money is filed in a court of competent jurisdiction and enforced as a money order; other decisions are filed in a Magistrates Court (minor civil disputes) or the Supreme Court.
GIVEN `the kind of decision` IS A `Kind of decision to enforce` @desc What decision you want to enforce. One of: the final decision in the proceeding; an interim order under s 58; an injunction under s 59; a monetary decision that is not part of the final decision; another decision
      `it requires payment to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the part of the decision you want to enforce requires a person to pay an amount of money
      `it is a minor civil dispute` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision was made in a minor civil dispute
GIVETH A `How I enforce it`
`How do I enforce the tribunal's decision` MEANS
    IF `the kind of decision` EQUALS `another decision`
    THEN `not covered: part 7, division 4 enforces final decisions, interim orders, injunctions and monetary decisions (s 129)`
    ELSE BRANCH
            IF `it requires payment to me` OR (`the kind of decision` EQUALS `a monetary decision that is not part of the final decision`)
            THEN `file a copy of the decision in the registry of a court of competent jurisdiction; it is then a money order of that court and is enforced as one (s 131)`
            IF `it is a minor civil dispute`
            THEN `file a copy of the decision in the registry of a Magistrates Court; it is then a non-money order of that court and is enforced as one (s 132(2), (3), (6)(a))`
            OTHERWISE `file a copy of the decision in the registry of the Supreme Court; it is then a non-money order of that court and is enforced as one (s 132(2), (3), (6)(b))`

-- s 130 "The Limitation of Actions Act 1974, section 10(4) applies in relation to a final decision of the tribunal ... as
-- if the decision were a judgment becoming enforceable when the decision takes effect under this Act." Limitation of
-- Actions Act 1974 s 10(4) (deposited under its own subject): "An action shall not be brought upon a judgment after the
-- expiration of 12 years from the date on which the judgment becomes enforceable." The 12 years are brought in as the
-- number s 130 uses (fork F11); the last day is the anniversary 12 years on (fork F12).
@export What is the last day I can bring an action on a final decision of the Queensland Civil and Administrative Tribunal, under the QCAT Act 2009 s 130 and the Limitation of Actions Act 1974 s 10(4)? 12 years from the day the decision takes effect.
GIVEN `the day it was made` IS A DATE @desc The day the decision was made; ISO 8601
      `it states a later day` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision states a later day on which it takes effect
      `the later day` IS A DATE @desc That later day (any date if none)
GIVETH A DATE
`What is the last day to bring an action on the decision` MEANS
    `the date` 12 `years after` (`s 127 -- the day the decision takes effect` `the day it was made` `it states a later day` `the later day`)

-- s 133(1) "This section applies if-- (a) it is not possible for the tribunal's final decision ... to be complied with;
-- or (b) there are problems with interpreting, implementing or enforcing the tribunal's final decision". (2) "A party ...
-- may apply ... for a renewal". (3) form, reasons, within the period in the rules, filed. (6) "A party can not make an
-- application under this section in relation to a final decision the subject of an appeal, or an application for leave
-- to appeal, under part 8." (7) "whether or not the appeal or application has been decided." s 134(4) "The tribunal's
-- final decision can not be renewed again".
DECLARE `Can I apply to renew the decision` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you may apply to the tribunal to renew the final decision, in the approved form, stating your reasons, filed within the period in the rules, with a copy to each other party (s 133(2)-(4)); the tribunal may make the same decision or another it could have made (s 134(2))`
    `no: renewal is only where the decision cannot be complied with, or there are problems interpreting, implementing or enforcing it (s 133(1))`
    `no: the decision is the subject of an appeal or an application for leave to appeal, decided or not (s 133(6), (7))`
    `no: a renewed final decision cannot be renewed again (s 134(4))`

@export Can I apply to the Queensland Civil and Administrative Tribunal to renew its final decision because it cannot be complied with, or is hard to interpret, implement or enforce, under the QCAT Act 2009 ss 133 and 134? Not if the decision has been appealed (or leave to appeal sought), and only once.
GIVEN `it cannot be complied with` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is not possible for the final decision to be complied with
      `there are problems with it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether there are problems with interpreting, implementing or enforcing the final decision
      `it has been appealed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision is, or was, the subject of an appeal or an application for leave to appeal under part 8 (decided or not)
      `it is already a renewed decision` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision is itself a renewal of an earlier final decision
GIVETH A `Can I apply to renew the decision`
`Can I apply to renew the decision` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`it cannot be complied with` OR `there are problems with it`)
        THEN `no: renewal is only where the decision cannot be complied with, or there are problems interpreting, implementing or enforcing it (s 133(1))`
        IF `it has been appealed`
        THEN `no: the decision is the subject of an appeal or an application for leave to appeal, decided or not (s 133(6), (7))`
        IF `it is already a renewed decision`
        THEN `no: a renewed final decision cannot be renewed again (s 134(4))`
        OTHERWISE `yes: you may apply to the tribunal to renew the final decision, in the approved form, stating your reasons, filed within the period in the rules, with a copy to each other party (s 133(2)-(4)); the tribunal may make the same decision or another it could have made (s 134(2))`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 136-141 -- reopening` -- arises only if the tribunal decided your proceeding and you missed the hearing or have new evidence
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 136 "This division applies to a proceeding, other than an appeal under part 8, division 1, that has been heard and
-- decided". s 138(1) "A party ... may apply ... for the proceeding to be reopened if the party considers a reopening
-- ground exists". Schedule 3: "reopening ground ... means-- (a) the party did not appear at the hearing of the proceeding
-- and had a reasonable excuse for not attending the hearing; or (b) the party would suffer a substantial injustice if the
-- proceeding was not reopened because significant new evidence has arisen and that evidence was not reasonably available
-- when the proceeding was first heard and decided." s 138(2)(b) within the period in the rules; (5) "A party can not
-- make an application ... in relation to a decision the subject of an appeal, or an application for leave to appeal";
-- (6) decided or not. s 139(4) the tribunal grants only if a ground exists and it "could be effectively or conveniently
-- dealt with by reopening"; (5) the decision on the application is final. s 140(6) "The proceeding can not be reopened
-- again". The tribunal's view of the ground is asked as the person's own (s 138(1) "considers").
DECLARE `Can I apply to reopen` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you did not appear at the hearing and had a reasonable excuse (schedule 3, reopening ground (a)); apply within the period in the rules, stating the ground, with the prescribed fee and a copy to each other party (s 138(2)-(4)); the tribunal grants it only if the ground exists and could be dealt with by reopening, and its decision is final (s 139(4), (5))`
    `yes: significant new evidence, not reasonably available when the proceeding was first heard and decided, means you would suffer a substantial injustice if it were not reopened (schedule 3, reopening ground (b)); apply within the period in the rules, stating the ground, with the prescribed fee and a copy to each other party (s 138(2)-(4)); the tribunal grants it only if the ground exists and could be dealt with by reopening, and its decision is final (s 139(4), (5))`
    `no: reopening is not available for an appeal to the appeal tribunal (s 136)`
    `no: the proceeding has not yet been heard and decided (s 136)`
    `no: you have no reopening ground: neither a reasonable excuse for missing the hearing, nor significant new evidence that was not reasonably available (s 138(1); schedule 3)`
    `no: the decision is the subject of an appeal or an application for leave to appeal, decided or not (s 138(5), (6))`
    `no: the proceeding has already been reopened once and cannot be reopened again (s 140(6))`

@export Can I apply to the Queensland Civil and Administrative Tribunal to reopen my proceeding after it was decided, under the QCAT Act 2009 ss 136-140 and the schedule 3 definition of reopening ground? Only if you missed the hearing with a reasonable excuse, or significant new evidence has arisen that was not reasonably available and you would suffer a substantial injustice; not after an appeal, and only once.
GIVEN `it is an appeal to the appeal tribunal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proceeding was itself an appeal to the QCAT appeal tribunal
      `it has been heard and decided` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the tribunal has heard and decided the proceeding (a compulsory conference at which the proceeding was decided counts as a hearing, s 137)
      `I missed the hearing with a reasonable excuse` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you did not appear at the hearing and had a reasonable excuse for not attending
      `there is significant new evidence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether significant new evidence has arisen that was not reasonably available when the proceeding was first heard and decided, so that you would suffer a substantial injustice if it were not reopened
      `it has been appealed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision is, or was, the subject of an appeal or an application for leave to appeal (decided or not)
      `it was reopened before` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proceeding has already been reopened once
GIVETH A `Can I apply to reopen`
`Can I apply to reopen my proceeding` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `it is an appeal to the appeal tribunal`
        THEN `no: reopening is not available for an appeal to the appeal tribunal (s 136)`
        IF NOT (`it has been heard and decided`)
        THEN `no: the proceeding has not yet been heard and decided (s 136)`
        IF `it was reopened before`
        THEN `no: the proceeding has already been reopened once and cannot be reopened again (s 140(6))`
        IF `it has been appealed`
        THEN `no: the decision is the subject of an appeal or an application for leave to appeal, decided or not (s 138(5), (6))`
        IF `I missed the hearing with a reasonable excuse`
        THEN `yes: you did not appear at the hearing and had a reasonable excuse (schedule 3, reopening ground (a)); apply within the period in the rules, stating the ground, with the prescribed fee and a copy to each other party (s 138(2)-(4)); the tribunal grants it only if the ground exists and could be dealt with by reopening, and its decision is final (s 139(4), (5))`
        IF `there is significant new evidence`
        THEN `yes: significant new evidence, not reasonably available when the proceeding was first heard and decided, means you would suffer a substantial injustice if it were not reopened (schedule 3, reopening ground (b)); apply within the period in the rules, stating the ground, with the prescribed fee and a copy to each other party (s 138(2)-(4)); the tribunal grants it only if the ground exists and could be dealt with by reopening, and its decision is final (s 139(4), (5))`
        OTHERWISE `no: you have no reopening ground: neither a reasonable excuse for missing the hearing, nor significant new evidence that was not reasonably available (s 138(1); schedule 3)`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 22, 51A, 138A, 145, 152 -- does the decision keep operating`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 22(1) "The start of a proceeding for the review of a reviewable decision ... does not affect the operation of the
-- decision or prevent the implementation of the decision." (2) unless an enabling Act provides otherwise or a stay order.
-- s 51A(1), (2) the same for an application to set aside a decision by default; s 138A(1), (2) for an application to
-- reopen; s 145(1), (2) for an appeal to the appeal tribunal; s 152(1), (2) for an appeal to the Court of Appeal. Each:
-- the decision keeps operating unless stayed. A stay of part only is outside this question (asked as stayed or not).
DECLARE `My challenge` IS ONE OF
    `an application for review of a reviewable decision`
    `the respondent's application to set aside a decision by default`
    `an application to reopen the proceeding`
    `an appeal to the appeal tribunal`
    `an appeal to the Court of Appeal`

@export Does the decision keep operating, and can it be acted on, while it is under review, a reopening application or an appeal, under the QCAT Act 2009 ss 22, 51A, 138A, 145 and 152? Yes, unless the tribunal (or the Court of Appeal) orders a stay. Answers nothing for a review where another Act provides otherwise.
GIVEN `the challenge` IS A `My challenge` @desc What is under way. One of: an application for review of a reviewable decision; the respondent's application to set aside a decision by default; an application to reopen the proceeding; an appeal to the appeal tribunal; an appeal to the Court of Appeal
      `a stay has been ordered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the tribunal or the Court of Appeal has ordered that the operation of the decision be stayed (and the stay is still in effect)
      `an enabling Act provides otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a review: whether the Act under which the decision was made provides otherwise about the decision's operation during a review
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does the decision keep operating` MEANS
    IF     (`the challenge` EQUALS `an application for review of a reviewable decision`)
       AND `an enabling Act provides otherwise`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST (NOT (`a stay has been ordered`))

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 141, 142, 143 -- appeals to the appeal tribunal` -- arises only if you want to challenge a decision of the tribunal
-- ===================================================================================================================

DECLARE `Kind of decision appealed` IS ONE OF
    `the final decision`
    `a decision that is not the final decision`
    `a costs order`
    `a cost-amount decision`
    `a decision under s 35 about accepting or rejecting an application`
    `a decision under s 51 to set aside a decision by default`
    `a decision under s 139 on an application to reopen`
    `the appeal tribunal's refusal of leave to appeal`
    `the appeal tribunal's final decision in an appeal`

DECLARE `Kind of question` IS ONE OF
    `a question of law only`
    `a question of fact, or of mixed law and fact`

-- s 142(1) "A party ... may appeal to the appeal tribunal against a decision of the tribunal ... if a judicial member did
-- not constitute the tribunal". (2) "However, a party ... can not appeal ... against-- (a) a decision under section 35;
-- (b) a decision to set aside a decision by default under section 51; (c) a cost-amount decision." (3) "(a) an appeal
-- ... against any of the following decisions ... may be made only if the party has obtained the appeal tribunal's leave
-- to appeal-- (i) a decision in a proceeding for a minor civil dispute; (ii) a decision that is not the tribunal's final
-- decision ...; (iii) a costs order; and (b) an appeal ... on a question of fact, or a question of mixed law and fact,
-- may be made only if the party has obtained the appeal tribunal's leave". s 139(5) "The tribunal's decision on the
-- application [to reopen] is final and can not be ... appealed against". s 141(2) "An appeal, or an application for
-- leave to appeal, against the final decision can not be made until the application under section 138 is finally dealt
-- with". Decisions of the appeal tribunal go to the Court of Appeal (s 150), not here.
DECLARE `Can I appeal to the appeal tribunal` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you may appeal to the appeal tribunal without leave (s 142(1))`
    `yes, but only with the appeal tribunal's leave: you must first apply for leave to appeal (s 142(3))`
    `no: a judicial member constituted the tribunal, so an appeal lies to the Court of Appeal instead (ss 142(1), 149(2))`
    `no: a decision under s 35, a decision under s 51 to set aside a decision by default, and a cost-amount decision cannot be appealed to the appeal tribunal (s 142(2)); a cost-amount decision may go to the Court of Appeal (s 149(1))`
    `no: the tribunal's decision on an application to reopen is final and cannot be appealed (s 139(5))`
    `no: a decision of the appeal tribunal is appealed to the Court of Appeal, not to the appeal tribunal (ss 142(1), 150)`
    `not yet: no appeal or application for leave to appeal against the final decision until your application to reopen is finally dealt with (s 141)`

@export Can I appeal to the QCAT appeal tribunal against a decision of the Queensland Civil and Administrative Tribunal, and do I need leave, under the QCAT Act 2009 ss 139(5), 141 and 142? You need leave for a minor civil dispute, a decision that is not final, a costs order, or a question of fact or mixed law and fact. If a judicial member decided it, appeal to the Court of Appeal instead.
GIVEN `a judicial member decided it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the tribunal was constituted by a judicial member (the president, the deputy president, or a judge or Land Court member sitting as a supplementary member, or a former judge nominated by the president)
      `the kind of decision` IS A `Kind of decision appealed` @desc What decision you want to appeal against. One of: the final decision; a decision that is not the final decision; a costs order; a cost-amount decision (the amount of costs fixed or assessed); a decision under s 35 about accepting or rejecting an application; a decision under s 51 to set aside a decision by default; a decision under s 139 on an application to reopen; the appeal tribunal's refusal of leave to appeal; the appeal tribunal's final decision in an appeal
      `it is a minor civil dispute` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision was made in a proceeding for a minor civil dispute
      `the kind of question` IS A `Kind of question` @desc What your appeal is about. One of: a question of law only; a question of fact, or of mixed law and fact
      `my reopening application is pending` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have applied to reopen the proceeding and that application has not yet been finally dealt with
GIVETH A `Can I appeal to the appeal tribunal`
`Can I appeal to the appeal tribunal` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF    (`the kind of decision` EQUALS `the appeal tribunal's refusal of leave to appeal`)
           OR (`the kind of decision` EQUALS `the appeal tribunal's final decision in an appeal`)
        THEN `no: a decision of the appeal tribunal is appealed to the Court of Appeal, not to the appeal tribunal (ss 142(1), 150)`
        IF `the kind of decision` EQUALS `a decision under s 139 on an application to reopen`
        THEN `no: the tribunal's decision on an application to reopen is final and cannot be appealed (s 139(5))`
        IF    (`the kind of decision` EQUALS `a decision under s 35 about accepting or rejecting an application`)
           OR (`the kind of decision` EQUALS `a decision under s 51 to set aside a decision by default`)
           OR (`the kind of decision` EQUALS `a cost-amount decision`)
        THEN `no: a decision under s 35, a decision under s 51 to set aside a decision by default, and a cost-amount decision cannot be appealed to the appeal tribunal (s 142(2)); a cost-amount decision may go to the Court of Appeal (s 149(1))`
        IF `a judicial member decided it`
        THEN `no: a judicial member constituted the tribunal, so an appeal lies to the Court of Appeal instead (ss 142(1), 149(2))`
        IF `my reopening application is pending` AND (`the kind of decision` EQUALS `the final decision`)
        THEN `not yet: no appeal or application for leave to appeal against the final decision until your application to reopen is finally dealt with (s 141)`
        IF    `it is a minor civil dispute`
           OR (`the kind of decision` EQUALS `a decision that is not the final decision`)
           OR (`the kind of decision` EQUALS `a costs order`)
           OR (`the kind of question` EQUALS `a question of fact, or of mixed law and fact`)
        THEN `yes, but only with the appeal tribunal's leave: you must first apply for leave to appeal (s 142(3))`
        OTHERWISE `yes: you may appeal to the appeal tribunal without leave (s 142(1))`

-- s 143(5) and s 151(3): "relevant day, for an application or appeal, means-- (a) if a person makes an application under
-- part 7, division 5, 6 or 7 about the decision being appealed against within 28 days after the person is given written
-- reasons for the decision--the day that application is finally dealt with under that division; or (b) if written
-- reasons have not been given for the decision being appealed against and reasons have not been requested under section
-- 122 or are not required to be given--the day the person received notice of the decision; or (c) the day the person is
-- given written reasons for the decision being appealed against." Part 7 divisions 5, 6, 7 are renewal, correcting
-- mistakes and reopening. Where reasons were requested and must be given but have not yet been, no paragraph gives a day
-- yet: NOTHING (finding 3). Where a division 5, 6 or 7 application made within the 28 days is not yet dealt with:
-- NOTHING.
DECLARE `My application to renew, correct or reopen` IS ONE OF
    `I made none within 28 days after getting written reasons`
    `I made one within 28 days after getting written reasons, and it has been finally dealt with`
    `I made one within 28 days after getting written reasons, and it is not yet finally dealt with`

GIVEN `I have written reasons` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day I got written reasons` IS A DATE
      `I asked for written reasons` IS A BOOLEAN
      `reasons are not required` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day I received notice` IS A DATE
      `my application to renew, correct or reopen` IS A `My application to renew, correct or reopen`
      `the day it was dealt with` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`ss 143(5), 151(3) -- the relevant day` `I have written reasons` `the day I got written reasons` `I asked for written reasons` `reasons are not required` `the day I received notice` `my application to renew, correct or reopen` `the day it was dealt with` MEANS
    IF `I have written reasons`
    THEN CONSIDER `my application to renew, correct or reopen`
         WHEN `I made one within 28 days after getting written reasons, and it has been finally dealt with` THEN JUST `the day it was dealt with`
         WHEN `I made one within 28 days after getting written reasons, and it is not yet finally dealt with` THEN NOTHING
         WHEN `I made none within 28 days after getting written reasons` THEN JUST `the day I got written reasons`
    ELSE IF (NOT (`I asked for written reasons`)) OR `reasons are not required`
         THEN JUST `the day I received notice`
         ELSE NOTHING

-- s 143(3) "An application for the appeal tribunal's leave to appeal must be filed in the registry within 28 days after
-- the relevant day." (4) "An appeal must be filed in the registry within-- (a) if the appeal tribunal's leave is required
-- for the appeal--21 days after the leave is given; or (b) otherwise--28 days after the relevant day." Note 1: an
-- enabling Act may set a different period (NOTHING here). Note 2: s 61 extension.
DECLARE `What I am filing` IS ONE OF
    `an application for leave to appeal`
    `an appeal that needs leave, after leave was given`
    `an appeal that does not need leave`

GIVEN `the enabling Act sets another period` IS A BOOLEAN
      `what I am filing` IS A `What I am filing`
      `the day leave was given` IS A DATE
      `I have written reasons` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day I got written reasons` IS A DATE
      `I asked for written reasons` IS A BOOLEAN
      `reasons are not required` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day I received notice` IS A DATE
      `my application to renew, correct or reopen` IS A `My application to renew, correct or reopen`
      `the day it was dealt with` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`s 143(3), (4) -- the last day to file` `the enabling Act sets another period` `what I am filing` `the day leave was given` `I have written reasons` `the day I got written reasons` `I asked for written reasons` `reasons are not required` `the day I received notice` `my application to renew, correct or reopen` `the day it was dealt with` MEANS
    IF `the enabling Act sets another period`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE CONSIDER `what I am filing`
         WHEN `an appeal that needs leave, after leave was given` THEN JUST (`the day` 21 `days after` `the day leave was given`)
         OTHERWISE
            CONSIDER `ss 143(5), 151(3) -- the relevant day` `I have written reasons` `the day I got written reasons` `I asked for written reasons` `reasons are not required` `the day I received notice` `my application to renew, correct or reopen` `the day it was dealt with`
            WHEN JUST `that day` THEN JUST (`the day` 28 `days after` `that day`)
            WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

@export By when must I file my appeal, or my application for leave to appeal, to the QCAT appeal tribunal, under the QCAT Act 2009 s 143(3)-(5)? Within 28 days after the relevant day (usually the day you get written reasons, or the day you got notice of the decision if no reasons were asked for), or, for an appeal that needs leave, within 21 days after leave is given. Answers nothing while requested reasons or a renewal, correction or reopening application are outstanding, or if another Act sets a different period.
GIVEN `the enabling Act sets another period` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an Act gives a different period for this appeal or application for leave
      `what I am filing` IS A `What I am filing` @desc What you are filing. One of: an application for leave to appeal; an appeal that needs leave, after leave was given; an appeal that does not need leave
      `the day leave was given` IS A DATE @desc The day the appeal tribunal gave you leave to appeal (any date if not relevant); ISO 8601
      `I have written reasons` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have been given written reasons for the decision
      `the day I got written reasons` IS A DATE @desc The day you were given written reasons (any date if not)
      `I asked for written reasons` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you asked the tribunal for written reasons under s 122
      `reasons are not required` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the tribunal is not required to give written reasons for this decision (s 122(4))
      `the day I received notice` IS A DATE @desc The day you received notice of the decision; ISO 8601
      `my application to renew, correct or reopen` IS A `My application to renew, correct or reopen` @desc Whether, within 28 days after getting written reasons, you applied to renew the decision, correct a mistake in it or reopen the proceeding. One of: I made none within 28 days after getting written reasons; I made one within 28 days after getting written reasons, and it has been finally dealt with; I made one within 28 days after getting written reasons, and it is not yet finally dealt with
      `the day it was dealt with` IS A DATE @desc The day that application was finally dealt with (any date if not relevant)
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`By when must I appeal to the appeal tribunal` MEANS
    `s 143(3), (4) -- the last day to file` `the enabling Act sets another period` `what I am filing` `the day leave was given` `I have written reasons` `the day I got written reasons` `I asked for written reasons` `reasons are not required` `the day I received notice` `my application to renew, correct or reopen` `the day it was dealt with`

-- The rule: I DO file my appeal or application for leave WITHIN the days from the start of the period to its last day.
-- Not appealing is not a wrong; the BREACH marks only that the time passed. s 61 extension as for a review.
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Party` `Act done`
`s 143(3), (4) -- filing an appeal or application for leave` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `I`
    DO `file my appeal or application for leave to appeal`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    LEST BREACH BY `I` BECAUSE "the period for filing the appeal or the application for leave to appeal has passed"

@export Am I in time to file my appeal, or my application for leave to appeal, to the QCAT appeal tribunal, under the QCAT Act 2009 ss 143 and 61? Answers nothing while requested reasons or a renewal, correction or reopening application are outstanding, or if another Act sets a different period.
GIVEN `the enabling Act sets another period` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an Act gives a different period for this appeal or application for leave
      `what I am filing` IS A `What I am filing` @desc What you are filing. One of: an application for leave to appeal; an appeal that needs leave, after leave was given; an appeal that does not need leave
      `the day leave was given` IS A DATE @desc The day the appeal tribunal gave you leave to appeal (any date if not relevant); ISO 8601
      `I have written reasons` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have been given written reasons for the decision
      `the day I got written reasons` IS A DATE @desc The day you were given written reasons (any date if not)
      `I asked for written reasons` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you asked the tribunal for written reasons under s 122
      `reasons are not required` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the tribunal is not required to give written reasons for this decision (s 122(4))
      `the day I received notice` IS A DATE @desc The day you received notice of the decision; ISO 8601
      `my application to renew, correct or reopen` IS A `My application to renew, correct or reopen` @desc Whether, within 28 days after getting written reasons, you applied to renew the decision, correct a mistake in it or reopen the proceeding. One of: I made none within 28 days after getting written reasons; I made one within 28 days after getting written reasons, and it has been finally dealt with; I made one within 28 days after getting written reasons, and it is not yet finally dealt with
      `the day it was dealt with` IS A DATE @desc The day that application was finally dealt with (any date if not relevant)
      `the day I file` IS A DATE @desc The day you file (or filed) the appeal or application in the registry; ISO 8601
      `the tribunal extended the time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the tribunal has ordered under s 61 that your time be extended
      `the extended last day` IS A DATE @desc The last day under the extension (any date if none)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Am I in time to appeal to the appeal tribunal` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 143(3), (4) -- the last day to file` `the enabling Act sets another period` `what I am filing` `the day leave was given` `I have written reasons` `the day I got written reasons` `I asked for written reasons` `reasons are not required` `the day I received notice` `my application to renew, correct or reopen` `the day it was dealt with`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
    WHEN JUST `the last day` THEN
        IF `the tribunal extended the time`
        THEN JUST (`the day` `the day I file` `is on or before` `the extended last day`)
        ELSE JUST (`done in time under` (`s 143(3), (4) -- filing an appeal or application for leave` `the period`) `file my appeal or application for leave to appeal` `at` (`the days from` `the start of the period` `to` `the day I file`))
        WHERE
            `the start of the period` MEANS
                CONSIDER `what I am filing`
                WHEN `an appeal that needs leave, after leave was given` THEN `the day leave was given`
                OTHERWISE `the day` (0 MINUS 28) `days after` `the last day`
            `the period` MEANS `the days from` `the start of the period` `to` `the last day`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 149, 150, 151 -- appeals to the Court of Appeal` -- arises only if a judicial member decided your matter, or after the appeal tribunal
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 149(1) "A party to a proceeding (other than an appeal under division 1) may appeal to the Court of Appeal against a
-- cost-amount decision ..., whether or not a judicial member constituted the tribunal". (2) "... may appeal ... against
-- another decision of the tribunal ... if a judicial member constituted the tribunal". (3) "(a) an appeal under
-- subsection (1) may be made only on a question of law and only if the party has obtained the court's leave to appeal;
-- and (b) an appeal under subsection (2) on a question of fact, or a question of mixed law and fact, may be made only if
-- the party has obtained the court's leave". (4) "a party ... can not appeal to the Court of Appeal against a decision of
-- the tribunal under section 35." s 150(1) "A person may appeal ... against a decision of the appeal tribunal to refuse
-- an application for leave to appeal". (2) a party to an appeal may appeal against "(a) a cost-amount decision; (b) the
-- final decision". (3) "only on a question of law; and ... only if the party has obtained the court's leave". s 139(5)
-- the decision on a reopening application is final. "only" and flat negatives: JUST FALSE kind. A tribunal decision
-- without a judicial member (other than a cost-amount decision) goes to the appeal tribunal: answered so. Another
-- decision of the appeal tribunal: s 150 lists without "only": "not covered".
DECLARE `Who made the decision` IS ONE OF
    `the tribunal, constituted by a judicial member`
    `the tribunal, not constituted by a judicial member`
    `the appeal tribunal`

DECLARE `Can I appeal to the Court of Appeal` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you may appeal to the Court of Appeal without leave, on a question of law (s 149(2))`
    `yes, but only with the Court of Appeal's leave (s 149(2), (3)(b))`
    `yes, but only on a question of law and only with the Court of Appeal's leave (ss 149(1), (3)(a), 150)`
    `no: this appeal may be made only on a question of law (ss 149(3)(a), 150(3)(a))`
    `no: a decision under s 35 cannot be appealed to the Court of Appeal (s 149(4))`
    `no: the tribunal's decision on an application to reopen is final and cannot be appealed (s 139(5))`
    `no: a judicial member did not constitute the tribunal, so the appeal lies to the appeal tribunal (ss 142(1), 149(2))`
    `not covered: s 150 provides for appeals from the appeal tribunal's refusal of leave, its cost-amount decisions and its final decision`

@export Can I appeal to the Queensland Court of Appeal against a decision of the Queensland Civil and Administrative Tribunal or its appeal tribunal, and do I need leave, under the QCAT Act 2009 ss 149 and 150? Decisions by a judicial member go straight to the Court of Appeal (leave needed on facts); cost-amount decisions and appeal tribunal decisions go only on a question of law, with leave.
GIVEN `who made it` IS A `Who made the decision` @desc Who made the decision. One of: the tribunal, constituted by a judicial member; the tribunal, not constituted by a judicial member; the appeal tribunal
      `the kind of decision` IS A `Kind of decision appealed` @desc What decision you want to appeal against. One of: the final decision; a decision that is not the final decision; a costs order; a cost-amount decision (the amount of costs fixed or assessed); a decision under s 35 about accepting or rejecting an application; a decision under s 51 to set aside a decision by default; a decision under s 139 on an application to reopen; the appeal tribunal's refusal of leave to appeal; the appeal tribunal's final decision in an appeal
      `the kind of question` IS A `Kind of question` @desc What your appeal is about. One of: a question of law only; a question of fact, or of mixed law and fact
GIVETH A `Can I appeal to the Court of Appeal`
`Can I appeal to the Court of Appeal` MEANS
    CONSIDER `who made it`
    WHEN `the appeal tribunal` THEN
        IF    (`the kind of decision` EQUALS `the appeal tribunal's refusal of leave to appeal`)
           OR (`the kind of decision` EQUALS `the appeal tribunal's final decision in an appeal`)
           OR (`the kind of decision` EQUALS `a cost-amount decision`)
        THEN `law only, with leave`
        ELSE `not covered: s 150 provides for appeals from the appeal tribunal's refusal of leave, its cost-amount decisions and its final decision`
    OTHERWISE
        BRANCH
            IF `the kind of decision` EQUALS `a decision under s 35 about accepting or rejecting an application`
            THEN `no: a decision under s 35 cannot be appealed to the Court of Appeal (s 149(4))`
            IF `the kind of decision` EQUALS `a decision under s 139 on an application to reopen`
            THEN `no: the tribunal's decision on an application to reopen is final and cannot be appealed (s 139(5))`
            IF `the kind of decision` EQUALS `a cost-amount decision`
            THEN `law only, with leave`
            IF `who made it` EQUALS `the tribunal, not constituted by a judicial member`
            THEN `no: a judicial member did not constitute the tribunal, so the appeal lies to the appeal tribunal (ss 142(1), 149(2))`
            IF `the kind of question` EQUALS `a question of fact, or of mixed law and fact`
            THEN `yes, but only with the Court of Appeal's leave (s 149(2), (3)(b))`
            OTHERWISE `yes: you may appeal to the Court of Appeal without leave, on a question of law (s 149(2))`
    WHERE
        `law only, with leave` MEANS
            IF `the kind of question` EQUALS `a question of law only`
            THEN `yes, but only on a question of law and only with the Court of Appeal's leave (ss 149(1), (3)(a), 150)`
            ELSE `no: this appeal may be made only on a question of law (ss 149(3)(a), 150(3)(a))`

-- s 151(2) "The application or appeal must be made-- (a) under the Uniform Civil Procedure Rules 1999; and (b) within 28
-- days after the relevant day unless the Court of Appeal orders otherwise." (3) relevant day as in s 143(5). An order
-- of the Court of Appeal is asked (NOTHING when made).
@export By when must I appeal, or apply for leave to appeal, to the Queensland Court of Appeal against a decision of the Queensland Civil and Administrative Tribunal, under the QCAT Act 2009 s 151? Within 28 days after the relevant day (usually the day you get written reasons, or the day you got notice of the decision if no reasons were asked for), unless the Court of Appeal orders otherwise. Answers nothing while requested reasons or a renewal, correction or reopening application are outstanding, or if the Court has ordered another time.
GIVEN `the court ordered otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court of Appeal has ordered a different time
      `I have written reasons` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have been given written reasons for the decision
      `the day I got written reasons` IS A DATE @desc The day you were given written reasons (any date if not)
      `I asked for written reasons` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you asked the tribunal for written reasons under s 122
      `reasons are not required` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the tribunal is not required to give written reasons for this decision (s 122(4))
      `the day I received notice` IS A DATE @desc The day you received notice of the decision; ISO 8601
      `my application to renew, correct or reopen` IS A `My application to renew, correct or reopen` @desc Whether, within 28 days after getting written reasons, you applied to renew the decision, correct a mistake in it or reopen the proceeding. One of: I made none within 28 days after getting written reasons; I made one within 28 days after getting written reasons, and it has been finally dealt with; I made one within 28 days after getting written reasons, and it is not yet finally dealt with
      `the day it was dealt with` IS A DATE @desc The day that application was finally dealt with (any date if not relevant)
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`By when must I appeal to the Court of Appeal` MEANS
    IF `the court ordered otherwise`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE CONSIDER `ss 143(5), 151(3) -- the relevant day` `I have written reasons` `the day I got written reasons` `I asked for written reasons` `reasons are not required` `the day I received notice` `my application to renew, correct or reopen` `the day it was dealt with`
         WHEN JUST `that day` THEN JUST (`the day` 28 `days after` `that day`)
         WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
